    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/arcana",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Arcana",
      "slug": "arcana",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ARCANA",
        "lineStart": 247,
        "lineEnd": 249,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Arcana is the domain of innate and instinctual magic. Those who choose this path tap into the raw, enigmatic forces of the realms to manipulate both their own energy and the elements. Arcana offers wielders a volatile power, but it is incredibly potent when correctly channeled. The Arcana domain can be accessed by the Druid and Sorcerer classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/arcana/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/blade",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Blade",
      "slug": "blade",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "BLADE",
        "lineStart": 251,
        "lineEnd": 253,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Blade is the domain of weapon mastery. Whether by steel, bow, or perhaps a more specialized arm, those who follow this path have the skill to cut short the lives of others. Wielders of Blade dedicate themselves to achieving inexorable power over death. The Blade domain can be accessed by the Assassin, Guardian, and Warrior classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/blade/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/bone",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Bone",
      "slug": "bone",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "BONE",
        "lineStart": 255,
        "lineEnd": 257,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Bone is the domain of tactics and the body. Practitioners of this domain have an uncanny control over their own physical abilities and an eye for predicting the behaviors of others in combat. Adherents to Bone gain an unparalleled understanding of bodies and their movements. The Bone domain can be accessed by the Brawler, Ranger, and Warrior classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/bone/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/codex",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Codex",
      "slug": "codex",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "CODEX",
        "lineStart": 259,
        "lineEnd": 261,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Codex is the domain of intensive magical study. Those who seek magical knowledge turn to the equations of power recorded in books, written on scrolls, etched into walls, or tattooed on bodies. Codex offers a commanding and versatile understanding of magic to devotees who pursue knowledge beyond the boundaries of common wisdom. The Codex domain can be accessed by the Bard and Wizard classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/codex/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/dread",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Dread",
      "slug": "dread",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DREAD",
        "lineStart": 263,
        "lineEnd": 265,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Dread is the domain of nightmares and fear. Those who choose this path can call forth monstrosities, enfeeble their foes, and channel terrifying magic to destroy their enemies. Dread grants its adherents power over forces most are too afraid to employ. The Dread domain can be accessed by the Warlock and Witch classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/dread/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/grace",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Grace",
      "slug": "grace",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GRACE",
        "lineStart": 267,
        "lineEnd": 269,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Grace is the domain of charisma. Through rapturous storytelling, charming spells, or a shroud of lies, those who channel this power define the realities of their adversaries, bending perception to their will. Grace offers its wielders raw magnetism and mastery over language. The Grace domain can be accessed by the Bard, Rogue, and Warlock classes",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/grace/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/midnight",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Midnight",
      "slug": "midnight",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "MIDNIGHT",
        "lineStart": 271,
        "lineEnd": 273,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Midnight is the domain of shadows and secrecy. Whether by clever tricks, deft magic, or the cloak of night, those who channel these forces practice the art of obscurity and can uncover sequestered treasures. Midnight offers practitioners the power to control and create enigmas. The Midnight domain can be accessed by the Assassin, Rogue, and Sorcerer classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/midnight/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/sage",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Sage",
      "slug": "sage",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SAGE",
        "lineStart": 275,
        "lineEnd": 277,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Sage is the domain of the natural world. Those who walk this path tap into the unfettered power of the earth and its creatures to unleash raw magic. Sage grants its adherents the vitality of a blooming flower and the ferocity of a ravenous predator. The Sage domain can be accessed by the Druid, Ranger, and Witch classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/sage/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/splendor",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Splendor",
      "slug": "splendor",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SPLENDOR",
        "lineStart": 279,
        "lineEnd": 281,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Splendor is the domain of life. Through this magic, followers gain the ability to heal and, to an extent, control death. Splendor offers its disciples the magnificent ability to both give and end life. The Splendor domain can be accessed by the Seraph and Wizard classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/splendor/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/valor",
      "@type": "Domain",
      "name": "Valor",
      "slug": "valor",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "VALOR",
        "lineStart": 283,
        "lineEnd": 285,
        "pdfPageStart": 7,
        "pdfPageEnd": 7
      },
      "description": "Valor is the domain of protection. Whether through attack or defense, those who choose this discipline channel formidable strength to protect their allies in battle. Valor offers great power to those who raise their shields in defense of others. The Valor domain can be accessed by the Brawler, Guardian, and Seraph classes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/domains/valor/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/assassin",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Assassin",
      "slug": "assassin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ASSASSIN",
        "lineStart": 374,
        "lineEnd": 396,
        "pdfPageStart": 9,
        "pdfPageEnd": 9
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/blade"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/midnight"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/executioners-guild"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/poisoners-guild"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 12,
      "startingHitPoints": 5,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Deadly Determination",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to clear 2 Stress."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Marked for Death",
          "rulesText": "On a successful weapon attack, you can mark a Stress to make the target Marked for Death. When you deal damage to a target you’ve Marked for Death, add a number of d4s equal to your tier to the damage roll.\n\nYou can have only one adversary Marked for Death at a time. This condition lasts until you take a rest, the current adversary Marked for Death is defeated, or the GM spends a number of Fear equal to your tier to clear it."
        },
        {
          "name": "Get In & Get Out",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Hope to ask the GM for a quick or inconspicuous way into or out of a place you can see. The next roll you make that acts on this information has advantage."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A list of names with several marked off or a rusted blade inscribed with an insignia",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "You once killed someone you were close to. What happened, and how did it change you?",
        "What organization trained you in the art of killing, and how did you become a member?",
        "Throughout your career, one target has eluded you. Who are they, and how have they slipped through your fingers?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "I’ve killed someone for you. Who were they?",
        "How did you save me when I was on the brink of death? What have I promised you as repayment?",
        "What secret about myself did I tell you, and how did it change your view of me?"
      ],
      "description": "Assassins are masters at inflicting deadly injuries with precise strikes. Unlike those who wield violence as only a means to an end, assassins approach death as a profession. Many members of this class believe theirs is a worthy, if not sacred, trade, and some join guilds to hone their craft, define their beliefs, and earn money. People from all walks of life hire assassins for their skills: powerful rulers looking to avoid all-out war, business leaders seeking to eliminate the competition, and even average people hoping to settle a grudge. Often, an assassin is the last resort for killing those previously believed to be unkillable. While some of these deadly professionals will destroy anyone in their path for the right reasons or the right price, others hold strict moral codes or personal rules that dictate their targets. Those who end up the target of an assassin should count themselves among the dead\n\nDOMAINS – Blade & Midnight",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/assassin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/bard",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Bard",
      "slug": "bard",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "BARD",
        "lineStart": 483,
        "lineEnd": 501,
        "pdfPageStart": 10,
        "pdfPageEnd": 10
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/codex"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/grace"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/troubadour"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/wordsmith"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 10,
      "startingHitPoints": 5,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Make a Scene",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to temporarily Distract a target within Close range, giving them a -2 penalty to their Difficulty."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Rally",
          "rulesText": "Once per session, describe how you rally the party and give yourself and each of your allies a Rally Die. At level 1, your Rally Die is a d6. A PC can spend their Rally Die to roll it, adding the result to their action roll, reaction roll, damage roll, or to clear a number of Stress equal to the result. At the end of each session, clear all unspent Rally Dice. At level 5, your Rally Die increases to a d8."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A romance novel or a letter never opened",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Who from your community taught you to have such confidence in yourself?",
        "You were in love once. Who did you adore, and how did they hurt you?",
        "You’ve always looked up to another bard. Who are they, and why do you idolize them?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What made you realize we were going to be such good friends?",
        "What do I do that annoys you?",
        "Why do you grab my hand at night?"
      ],
      "description": "Bards are the most charismatic people in all the realms. Members of this class are masters of captivation and specialize in a variety of performance types, including singing, playing musical instruments, weaving tales, or telling jokes. Whether performing for an audience or speaking to an individual, bards thrive in social situations. Members of this profession bond and train at schools or guilds, but a current of egotism runs through those of the bardic persuasion. While they may be the most likely class to bring people together, a bard of ill temper can just as easily tear a party apart.\n\nDOMAINS – Grace & Codex",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/bard/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/brawler",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Brawler",
      "slug": "brawler",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "BRAWLER",
        "lineStart": 575,
        "lineEnd": 599,
        "pdfPageStart": 11,
        "pdfPageEnd": 12
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/valor"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/bone"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/juggernaut"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/martial-artist"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 10,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Square Up",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to intimidate a target within Close range, making them temporarily Vulnerable."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "I Am the Weapon",
          "rulesText": "Your barehanded attacks are as strong as any blade. You have a primary weapon called Brawler’s Strike equipped while you have no other Active Weapons. It uses a trait of your choice, has Melee range, and deals d8+d6 physical damage using your Proficiency (both the d8 and d6 scale off your Proficiency). While this weapon is active, you gain a +1 bonus to your Evasion."
        },
        {
          "name": "Combo Strike",
          "rulesText": "After rolling damage on a successful attack with a Melee weapon, you can mark a Stress to start a combo strike. When you do, roll your Combo Die and note the result, then continue rolling your Combo Die until the result of your latest roll is lower than the roll that preceded it. You deal extra damage equal to the total of all rolled Combo Die results on this attack. The results can’t be modified by any means.\n\nYour Combo Die starts as a d4. Once per tier, you can increase your Combo Die by one step as a level advancement option."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "Hand wraps from a mentor or a book about your secret hobby",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Where did you spend time during your formative years that taught you, directly or indirectly, how to fight in the style you use?",
        "What organization has vowed to kill you on sight, and what did you do to invoke their ire?",
        "Who did you recently lose a fight to that you’re desperate for a rematch against?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What is one thing we’re both afraid of?",
        "What do I rely on you for during our travels? How do you feel about it?",
        "I still haven’t forgiven you for something you said to me. What was it, and why did you say it?"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "name": "Martial Stances",
          "rulesText": "When you choose the Martial Artist subclass, take the Martial Stances sheet to track which stances your character knows. You can also track which stance you have active and your current Focus."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stances",
          "rulesText": "Stances are special body positionings that enable Martial Artists to attack, react, or defend in combat. Each stance grants your character a different ability, bonus, or feature while active.\n\nYou choose two stances from Tier 1 when you first take the Martial Artist’s subclass foundation card. Mark the circle next to each to indicate that your character knows and has access to them. Mark a new stance from your tier or below each time you gain a level."
        },
        {
          "name": "Focus",
          "rulesText": "Focus represents your character’s poise, clarity, and control. Once per rest during a moment of calm, you can clear your mind and refocus your martial instincts. Clear your Focus track, then roll a number of d6s equal to your Instinct and gain Focus equal to the highest result rolled. You can hold a maximum of 6 Focus."
        },
        {
          "name": "Shifting Into Stances",
          "rulesText": "You can spend a Focus to shift into a martial stance and gain its effects. You can shift only into stances you’ve marked as known. When you shift into a stance, it’s considered your active stance until you drop out of it.\n\nYou can’t shift into or have more than one active stance at a time. Until you shift into a stance, you are not considered to be in a stance or to have an active stance."
        },
        {
          "name": "Dropping Out of Stances",
          "rulesText": "If you are already in a stance when you shift into a different stance, you automatically drop out of the previously active stance. You also drop out of your active stance when you take Severe damage or mark your last Hit Point. Otherwise, you drop out of your active stance at the end of the scene. When you drop out of a stance, you lose any ongoing benefits it provides."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stance Features",
          "rulesText": "The following section lists all martial stances by tier."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Experts in unarmed combat, brawlers hone their bodies into lethal weapons. Whether they learned from formal training, studied with a mentor, or picked up their skills one fight at a time, the process is always rigorous as brawlers develop their body and mind to work as one. Brawlers are valued for their power and versatility and typically join a party or a cause when the need or desire arises. Because a brawler’s body is their strongest weapon, they typically seek out new challenges or consistent sparring partners so they can maintain their skills and add new techniques to their repertoire. Though they might appear unassuming to those accustomed to foes who are armed to the teeth, brawlers often accomplish more with bare knuckles than an average soldier with a sword.\n\nDOMAINS – Valor & Bone",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/brawler/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/druid",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Druid",
      "slug": "druid",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DRUID",
        "lineStart": 737,
        "lineEnd": 757,
        "pdfPageStart": 14,
        "pdfPageEnd": 14
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/arcana"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/sage"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/warden-of-the-elements"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/warden-of-renewal"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 10,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Evolution",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to transform into a Beastform without marking a Stress. When you do, choose one trait to raise by +1 until you drop out of that Beastform."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Beastform",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to magically transform into a creature of your tier or lower from the Beastform list. You can drop out of this form at any time. While transformed, you can’t use weapons or cast spells from domain cards, but you can still use other features or abilities you have access to. Spells you cast before you transform stay active and last for their normal duration, and you can talk and communicate as normal. Additionally, you gain the Beastform’s features, add their Evasion bonus to your Evasion, and use the trait specified in their statistics for your attack. While you’re in a Beastform, your armor becomes part of your body and you mark Armor Slots as usual; when you drop out of a Beastform, those marked Armor Slots remain marked. If you mark your last Hit Point, you automatically drop out of this form."
        },
        {
          "name": "Wildtouch",
          "rulesText": "You can perform harmless, subtle effects that involve nature—such as causing a flower to rapidly grow, summoning a slight gust of wind, or starting a campfire— at will."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A small bag of rocks and bones or a strange pendant found in the dirt",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Why was the community you grew up in so reliant on nature and its creatures?",
        "Who was the first wild animal you bonded with? Why did your bond end?",
        "Who has been trying to hunt you down? What do they want from you?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What did you confide in me that makes me leap into danger for you every time?",
        "What animal do I say you remind me of?",
        "What affectionate nickname have you given me?"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "name": "Agile Scout",
          "rulesText": "(Fox, Mouse, Weasel, etc.)\n\nAgility +1 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Agility d4 phy\n\nGain advantage on: deceive, locate, sneak\n\nAgile: Your movement is silent, and you can spend a Hope to move up to Far range without rolling.\n\nFragile: When you take Major or greater damage, you drop out of Beastform."
        },
        {
          "name": "Household Friend",
          "rulesText": "(Cat, Dog, Rabbit, etc.)\n\nInstinct +1 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Instinct d6 phy\n\nGain advantage on: climb, locate, protect\n\nCompanion: When you Help an Ally, you can roll a d8 as your advantage die.\n\nFragile: When you take Major or greater damage, you drop out of Beastform."
        },
        {
          "name": "Nimble Grazer",
          "rulesText": "(Deer, Gazelle, Goat, etc.)\n\nAgility +1 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Agility d6 phy\n\nGain advantage on: leap, sneak, sprint\n\nElusive Prey: When an attack roll against you would succeed, you can mark a Stress and roll a d4. Add the result to your Evasion against this attack.\n\nFragile: When you take Major or greater damage, you drop out of Beastform."
        },
        {
          "name": "Pack Predator",
          "rulesText": "(Coyote, Hyena, Wolf, etc.)\n\nStrength +2 | Evasion +1\n\nMelee Strength d8+2 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, sprint, track\n\nHobbling Strike: When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can mark a Stress to make the target temporarily Vulnerable.\n\nPack Hunting: When you succeed on an attack against the same target as an ally who acts immediately before you, add a d8 to your damage roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Aquatic Scout",
          "rulesText": "(Eel, Fish, Octopus, etc.)\n\nAgility +1 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Agility d4 phy\n\nGain advantage on: navigate, sneak, swim\n\nAquatic: You can breathe and move naturally underwater.\n\nFragile: When you take Major or greater damage, you drop out of Beastform."
        },
        {
          "name": "Stalking Arachnid",
          "rulesText": "(Tarantula, Wolf Spider, etc.)\n\nFinesse +1 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Finesse d6+1 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, climb, sneak\n\nVenomous Bite: When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, the target becomes temporarily Poisoned. A Poisoned creature takes 1d10 direct physical damage each time they act.\n\nWebslinger: You can create a strong web material useful for both adventuring and battle. The web is resilient enough to support one creature. You can temporarily Restrain a target within Close range by succeeding on a Finesse Roll against them."
        },
        {
          "name": "Armored Sentry",
          "rulesText": "(Armadillo, Pangolin, Turtle, etc.)\n\nStrength +1 | Evasion +1\n\nMelee Strength d8+2 phy\n\nGain advantage on: dig, locate, protect\n\nArmored Shell: Your hardened exterior gives you resistance to physical damage. Additionally, mark an Armor Slot to retract into your shell. While in your shell, physical damage is reduced by a number equal to your Armor Score (after applying resistance), but you can’t perform other actions without leaving this form.\n\nCannonball: Mark a Stress to allow an ally to throw or launch you at an adversary. To do so, the ally makes an attack roll using Agility or Strength (their choice) against a target within Close range. On a success, the adversary takes d12+2 physical damage using the thrower’s Proficiency. You can spend a Hope to target an additional adversary within Very Close range of the first. The second target takes half the damage dealt to the first target."
        },
        {
          "name": "Powerful Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Bear, Bull, Moose, etc.)\n\nStrength +3 | Evasion +1\n\nMelee Strength d10+4 phy\n\nGain advantage on: navigate, protect, scare\n\nRampage: When you roll a 1 on a damage die, you can roll a d10 and add the result to the damage roll. Additionally, before you make an attack roll, you can mark a Stress to gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for that attack.\n\nThick Hide: You gain a +2 bonus to your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Mighty Strider",
          "rulesText": "(Camel, Horse, Zebra, etc.)\n\nAgility +1 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Agility d8+1 phy\n\nGain advantage on: leap, navigate, sprint\n\nCarrier: You can carry up to two willing allies with you when you move.\n\nTrample: Mark a Stress to move up to Close range in a straight line and make an attack against all targets within Melee range of the line. Targets you succeed against take d8+1 physical damage using your Proficiency and are temporarily Vulnerable."
        },
        {
          "name": "Striking Serpent",
          "rulesText": "(Cobra, Rattlesnake, Viper, etc.)\n\nFinesse +1 | Evasion +2\n\nVery Close Finesse d8+4 phy\n\nGain advantage on: climb, deceive, sprint\n\nVenomous Strike: Make an attack against any number of targets within Very Close range. On a success, a target is temporarily Poisoned. A Poisoned creature takes 1d10 direct physical damage each time they act.\n\nWarning Hiss: Mark a Stress to force any number of targets within Melee range to move back to Very Close range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Pouncing Predator",
          "rulesText": "(Cheetah, Lion, Panther, etc.)\n\nInstinct +1 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Instinct d8+6 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, climb, sneak\n\nFleet: Spend a Hope to move up to Far range without rolling.\n\nTakedown: Mark a Stress to move into Melee range of a target and make an attack roll against them. On a success, you gain a +2 bonus to your Proficiency for this attack and the target must mark a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Winged Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Hawk, Owl, Raven, etc.)\n\nFinesse +1 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Finesse d4+2 phy\n\nGain advantage on: deceive, locate, scare\n\nBird’s-Eye View: You can fly at will. Once per rest while you are airborne, you can ask the GM a question about the scene below you without needing to roll. The first time a character makes a roll to act on this information, they gain advantage on the roll.\n\nHollow Bones: You gain a −2 penalty to your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Great Predator",
          "rulesText": "(Dire Wolf, Velociraptor, Sabertooth Tiger, etc.)\n\nStrength +2 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Strength d12+8 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, sneak, sprint\n\nCarrier: You can carry up to two willing allies with you when you move.\n\nVicious Maul: When you succeed on an attack against a target, you can spend a Hope to make them temporarily Vulnerable and gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for this attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Mighty Lizard",
          "rulesText": "(Alligator, Crocodile, Gila Monster, etc.)\n\nInstinct +2 | Evasion +1\n\nMelee Instinct d10+7 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, sneak, track\n\nPhysical Defense: You gain a +3 bonus to your damage thresholds.\n\nSnapping Strike: When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can spend a Hope to clamp that opponent in your jaws, making them temporarily Restrained and Vulnerable."
        },
        {
          "name": "Great Winged Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Giant Eagle, Falcon, etc.)\n\nFinesse +2 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Finesse d8+6 phy\n\nGain advantage on: deceive, distract, locate\n\nBird’s-Eye View: You can fly at will. Once per rest while you are airborne, you can ask the GM a question about the scene below you without needing to roll. The first time a character makes a roll to act on this information, they gain advantage on the roll.\n\nCarrier: You can carry up to two willing allies with you when you move."
        },
        {
          "name": "Aquatic Predator",
          "rulesText": "(Dolphin, Orca, Shark, etc.)\n\nAgility +2 | Evasion +4\n\nMelee Agility d10+6 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, swim, track\n\nAquatic: You can breathe and move naturally underwater.\n\nVicious Maul: When you succeed on an attack against a target, you can spend a Hope to make them Vulnerable and gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for this attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Legendary Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Upgraded Tier 1 Options)\n\nEvolved: Pick a Tier 1 Beastform option and become a larger, more powerful version of that creature. While you’re in this form, you retain all traits and features from the original form and gain the following bonuses:\n\n- A +6 bonus to damage rolls\n- A +1 bonus to the trait used by this form\n- A +2 bonus to Evasion"
        },
        {
          "name": "Legendary Hybrid",
          "rulesText": "(Griffon, Sphinx, etc.)\n\nStrength +2 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Strength d10+8 phy\n\nHybrid Features: To transform into this creature, mark an additional Stress. Choose any two Beastform options from Tiers 1–2. Choose a total of four advantages and two features from those options."
        },
        {
          "name": "Massive Behemoth",
          "rulesText": "(Elephant, Mammoth, Rhinoceros, etc.)\n\nStrength +3 | Evasion +1\n\nMelee Strength d12+12 phy\n\nGain advantage on: locate, protect, scare, sprint\n\nCarrier: You can carry up to four willing allies with you when you move.\n\nDemolish: Spend a Hope to move up to Far range in a straight line and make an attack against all targets within Melee range of the line. Targets you succeed against take d8+10 physical damage using your Proficiency and are temporarily Vulnerable.\n\nUndaunted: You gain a +2 bonus to all your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Terrible Lizard",
          "rulesText": "(Brachiosaurus, Tyrannosaurus, etc.)\n\nStrength +3 | Evasion +2\n\nMelee Strength d12+10 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, deceive, scare, track\n\nDevastating Strikes: When you deal Severe damage to a target within Melee range, you can mark a Stress to force them to mark an additional Hit Point.\n\nMassive Stride: You can move up to Far range without rolling. You ignore rough terain (at the GM’s discretion) due to your size."
        },
        {
          "name": "Mythic Aerial Hunter",
          "rulesText": "(Dragon, Pterodactyl, Roc, Wyvern, etc.)\n\nFinesse +3 | Evasion +4\n\nMelee Finesse d10+11 phy\n\nGain advantage on: attack, deceive, locate, navigate\n\nCarrier: You can carry up to three willing allies with you when you move.\n\nDeadly Raptor: You can fly at will and move up to Far range as part of your action. When you move in a straight line into Melee range of a target from at least Close range and make an attack against that target in the same action, you can reroll all damage dice that rolled a result lower than your Proficiency."
        },
        {
          "name": "Epic Aquatic Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Giant Squid, Whale, etc.)\n\nAgility +3 | Evasion +3\n\nMelee Agility d10+10 phy\n\nGain advantage on: locate, protect, scare, track\n\nOcean Master: You can breathe and move naturally underwater. When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can temporarily Restrain them.\n\nUnyielding: When you would mark an Armor Slot, roll a d6. On a result of 5 or higher, reduce the severity by one threshold without marking an Armor Slot."
        },
        {
          "name": "Mythic Beast",
          "rulesText": "(Upgraded Tier 1 or Tier 2 Options)\n\nEvolved: Pick a Tier 1 or Tier 2 Beastform option and become a larger, more powerful version of that creature. While you’re in this form, you retain all traits and features from the original form and gain the following bonuses:\n\n- A +9 bonus to damage rolls\n- A +2 bonus to the trait used by this form\n- A +3 bonus to Evasion\n- Your damage die increases by one size (d6 becomes d8, d8 becomes d10, etc.)"
        },
        {
          "name": "Mythic Hybrid",
          "rulesText": "(Chimera, Cockatrice, Manticore, etc.)\n\nStrength +3 | Evasion +2 Strength Melee d12+10 phy\n\nHybrid Features: To transform into this creature, mark 2 additional Stress. Choose any three Beastform options from Tiers 1-3. Choose a total of five advantages and three features from those options."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Becoming a druid is more than an occupation; it’s a calling for those who wish to learn from and protect the magic of the wilderness. While one might underestimate a gentle druid who practices the often-quiet work of cultivating flora, druids who channel the untamed forces of nature are terrifying to behold. Druids cultivate their abilities in small groups, often connected by a specific ethos or locale, but some choose to work alone. Through years of study and dedication, druids can learn to transform into beasts and shape nature itself.\n\nDOMAINS – Sage & Arcana",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/druid/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/guardian",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Guardian",
      "slug": "guardian",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GUARDIAN",
        "lineStart": 1190,
        "lineEnd": 1216,
        "pdfPageStart": 19,
        "pdfPageEnd": 19
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/blade"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/valor"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/stalwart"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/vengeance"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 9,
      "startingHitPoints": 7,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Frontline Tank",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to clear 2 Armor Slots."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Unstoppable",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can become Unstoppable. You gain an Unstoppable Die. At level 1, your Unstoppable Die is a d4. Place it on your character sheet in the space provided, starting with the 1 value facing up. After you make a damage roll that deals 1 or more Hit Points to a target, increase the Unstoppable Die value by one. When the die’s value would exceed its maximum value or when the scene ends, remove the die and drop out of Unstoppable. At level 5, your Unstoppable Die increases to a d6.\n\nWhile Unstoppable, you gain the following benefits:\n\n  - You reduce the severity of physical damage by one threshold (Severe to Major, Major to Minor, Minor to None).\n  - You add the current value of the Unstoppable Die to your damage roll.\n  - You can’t be Restrained or Vulnerable."
        },
        {
          "name": "Tip",
          "rulesText": "If your Unstoppable Die is a d4 and the 4 is currently facing up, you remove the die the next time you would increase it. However, if your Unstoppable Die has increased to a d6 and the 4 is currently facing up, you’ll turn it to 5 the next time you would increase it. In this case, you’ll remove the die after you would need to increase it higher than 6."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A totem from your mentor or a secret key",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Who from your community did you fail to protect, and why do you still think of them?",
        "You’ve been tasked with protecting something important and delivering it somewhere dangerous. What is it, and where does it need to go?",
        "You consider an aspect of yourself to be a weakness. What is it, and how has it affected you?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "How did I save your life the first time we met?",
        "What small gift did you give me that you notice I always carry with me?",
        "What lie have you told me about yourself that I absolutely believe?"
      ],
      "description": "The title of guardian represents an array of martial professions, speaking more to their moral compass and unshakeable fortitude than the means by which they fight. While many guardians join groups of militants for either a country or cause, they’re more likely to follow those few they truly care for, majority be damned. Guardians are known for fighting with remarkable ferocity even against overwhelming odds, defending their cohort above all else. Woe betide those who harm the ally of a guardian, as the guardian will answer this injury in kind.\n\nDOMAINS – Valor & Blade",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/guardian/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/ranger",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Ranger",
      "slug": "ranger",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "RANGER",
        "lineStart": 1280,
        "lineEnd": 1302,
        "pdfPageStart": 20,
        "pdfPageEnd": 20
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/bone"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/sage"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/beastbound"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/wayfinder"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 12,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Hold Them Off",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope when you succeed on an attack with a weapon to use that same roll against two additional adversaries within range of the attack."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Ranger’s Focus",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Hope and make an attack against a target. On a success, deal your attack’s normal damage and temporarily make the attack’s target your Focus. Until this feature ends or you make a different creature your Focus, you gain the following benefits against your Focus:\n\n  - You know precisely what direction they are in.\n  - When you deal damage to them, they must mark a Stress.\n  - When you fail an attack against them, you can end your Ranger’s Focus feature to reroll your Duality Dice."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A trophy from your first kill or a seemingly broken compass",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "A terrible creature hurt your community, and you’ve vowed to hunt them down. What are they, and what unique trail or sign do they leave behind?",
        "Your first kill almost killed you, too. What was it, and what part of you was never the same after that event?",
        "You’ve traveled many dangerous lands, but what is the one place you refuse to go?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What friendly competition do we have?",
        "Why do you act differently when we’re alone than when others are around?",
        "What threat have you asked me to watch for, and why are you worried about it?"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "name": "Ranger Companion",
          "rulesText": "When you choose the Beastbound Ranger subclass, take a companion sheet. This sheet is for tracking important information about your character’s companion and can be tucked beneath the right side of your character sheet for ease of viewing."
        },
        {
          "name": "Step 1: Name Your Companion",
          "rulesText": "Work with the GM to decide what kind of animal you have as your companion. Give them a name and add a picture of them to the companion sheet."
        },
        {
          "name": "Step 2: Write Their Evasion",
          "rulesText": "Fill in their Evasion, which starts at 10."
        },
        {
          "name": "Step 3: Choose Their Companion Experience",
          "rulesText": "Create two Experiences for your companion based on their training and the history you have together.\n\nStart with +2 in both Experiences. Whenever you gain a new Experience, your companion also gains one. All new Experiences start at +2.\n\nExample Companion Experiences\n\nBold Distraction, Expert Climber, Fetch, Friendly, Guardian of the Forest, Horrifying, Intimidating, Loyal Until the End, Navigation, Nimble, Nobody Left Behind, On High Alert, Protective, Royal Companion, Scout, Service Animal, Trusted Mount, Vigilant, We Always Find Them, You Can’t Hit What You Can’t Find"
        },
        {
          "name": "Step 4: Choose Their Attack and Record Damage Die",
          "rulesText": "Finally, describe your companion’s method of dealing damage (their standard attack) and record it in the “Attack & Damage” section. At level 1, your companion’s damage die is a d6 and their range is Melee. Choose whether they deal physical or magic damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Working with Your Companion",
          "rulesText": "The following sections will run you through the basics of working with your companion."
        },
        {
          "name": "Using Spellcast Rolls, Hope, and Experiences",
          "rulesText": "Make a Spellcast Roll to connect with your companion and command them to take action. Spend a Hope to add an applicable Companion Experience to the roll. On a success with Hope, if your next action builds on their success, you gain advantage on the roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Attacking with Your Companion",
          "rulesText": "When you command your companion to attack, they gain any benefits that would normally only apply to you (such as the effects of “Ranger’s Focus”). On a success, their damage roll uses your Proficiency and their damage die."
        },
        {
          "name": "Taking Damage as Stress",
          "rulesText": "- When your companion would take any amount of damage, they mark a Stress. When they mark their last Stress, they drop out of the scene (by hiding, fleeing, or a similar action). They remain unavailable until the start of your next long rest, where they return with 1 Stress cleared.\n- When you choose a downtime move that clears Stress on yourself, your companion clears an equal number of Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Leveling Up Your Companion",
          "rulesText": "When your character levels up, choose one available option for your companion from the following list and mark it on your sheet:\n\n- Intelligent: Your companion gains a permanent +1 bonus to a Companion Experience of your choice.\n- Light in the Dark: Use this as an additional Hope slot your character can mark.\n- Creature Comfort: Once per rest, when you take time during a quiet moment to give your companion love and attention, you can gain a Hope or you can both clear a Stress.\n- Armored: When your companion takes damage, you can mark one of your Armor Slots instead of marking one of their Stress.\n- Vicious: Increase your companion’s damage dice or range by one step (d6 to d8, Close to Far, etc.).\n- Resilient: Your companion gains an additional Stress slot.\n- Bonded: When you mark your last Hit Point, your companion rushes to your side to comfort you. Roll a number of d6s equal to the unmarked Stress slots they have and mark them. If any roll a 6, your companion helps you up. Clear your last Hit Point and return to the scene.\n- Aware: Your companion gains a permanent +2 bonus to their Evasion."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Rangers are highly skilled hunters who, despite their martial abilities, rarely lend their skills to an army. Through mastery of the body and a deep understanding of the wilderness, rangers become sly tacticians, pursuing their quarry with cunning and patience. Many rangers track and fight alongside an animal companion with whom they’ve forged a powerful spiritual bond. By honing their skills in the wild, rangers become expert trackers, as likely to ensnare their foes in a trap as they are to assail them head-on.\n\nDOMAINS – Bone & Sage",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/ranger/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/rogue",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Rogue",
      "slug": "rogue",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ROGUE",
        "lineStart": 1432,
        "lineEnd": 1457,
        "pdfPageStart": 22,
        "pdfPageEnd": 22
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/grace"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/midnight"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/nightwalker"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/syndicate"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 12,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Rogue’s Dodge",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion until the next time an attack succeeds against you. Otherwise, this bonus lasts until your next rest."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Cloaked",
          "rulesText": "Any time you would be Hidden, you are instead Cloaked. In addition to the benefits of the Hidden condition, while Cloaked you remain unseen if you are stationary when an adversary moves to where they would normally see you. After you make an attack or end a move within line of sight of an adversary, you are no longer Cloaked."
        },
        {
          "name": "Sneak Attack",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an attack while Cloaked or while an ally is within Melee range of your target, add a number of d6s equal to your tier to your damage roll.\n\n- Level 1 → Tier 1\n- Levels 2–4 → Tier 2\n- Levels 5–7 → Tier 3\n- Levels 8–10 → Tier 4"
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A set of forgery tools or a grappling hook",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "What did you get caught doing that got you exiled from your home community?",
        "You used to have a different life, but you’ve tried to leave it behind. Who from your past is still chasing you?",
        "Who from your past were you most sad to say goodbye to?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What did I recently convince you to do that got us both in trouble?",
        "What have I discovered about your past that I hold secret from the others?",
        "Who do you know from my past, and how have they influenced your feelings about me?"
      ],
      "description": "Rogues are scoundrels, often in both attitude and practice. Broadly known as liars and thieves, the best among this class move through the world anonymously. Utilizing their sharp wits and blades, rogues trick their foes through social manipulation as easily as breaking locks, climbing through windows, or dealing underhanded blows. These masters of magical craft manipulate shadow and movement, adding an array of useful and deadly tools to their repertoire. Rogues frequently establish guilds to meet future accomplices, hire out jobs, and hone secret skills, proving that there’s honor among thieves for those who know where to look.\n\nDOMAINS – Midnight & Grace",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/rogue/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/seraph",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Seraph",
      "slug": "seraph",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SERAPH",
        "lineStart": 1540,
        "lineEnd": 1558,
        "pdfPageStart": 24,
        "pdfPageEnd": 24
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/splendor"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/valor"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/divine-wielder"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/winged-sentinel"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 9,
      "startingHitPoints": 7,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Life Support",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to clear a Hit Point on an ally within Close range."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Prayer Dice",
          "rulesText": "At the beginning of each session, roll a number of d4s equal to your subclass’s Spellcast trait and place them on your character sheet in the space provided. These are your Prayer Dice. You can spend any number of Prayer Dice to aid yourself or an ally within Far range. You can use a spent die’s value to reduce incoming damage, add to a roll’s result after the roll is made, or gain Hope equal to the result. At the end of each session, clear all unspent Prayer Dice."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A bundle of offerings or a sigil of your god",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Which god did you devote yourself to? What incredible feat did they perform for you in a moment of desperation?",
        "How did your appearance change after taking your oath?",
        "In what strange or unique way do you communicate with your god?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What promise did you make me agree to, should you die on the battlefield?",
        "Why do you ask me so many questions about my god?",
        "You’ve told me to protect one member of our party above all others, even yourself. Who are they and why?"
      ],
      "description": "Seraphs are divine fighters and healers imbued with sacred purpose. A wide array of deities exist within the realms, and thus numerous kinds of seraphs are appointed by these gods. Their ethos traditionally aligns with the domain or goals of their god, such as defending the weak, exacting vengeance, protecting a land or artifact, or upholding a particular faith. Some seraphs ally themselves with an army or locale, much to the satisfaction of their rulers, but other crusaders fight in opposition to the follies of the Mortal Realm. It is better to be a seraph’s ally than their enemy, as they are terrifying foes to those who defy their purpose.\n\nDOMAINS – Splendor & Valor",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/seraph/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/sorcerer",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Sorcerer",
      "slug": "sorcerer",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SORCERER",
        "lineStart": 1629,
        "lineEnd": 1654,
        "pdfPageStart": 25,
        "pdfPageEnd": 25
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/arcana"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/midnight"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/elemental-origin"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/primal-origin"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 10,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Volatile Magic",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to reroll any number of your damage dice on an attack that deals magic damage."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Arcane Sense",
          "rulesText": "You can sense the presence of magical people and objects within Close range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Minor Illusion",
          "rulesText": "Make a Spellcast Roll (10). On a success, you create a minor visual illusion no larger than yourself within Close range. This illusion is convincing to anyone at Close range or farther."
        },
        {
          "name": "Channel Raw Power",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can place a domain card from your loadout into your vault and choose to either:\n\n  - Gain Hope equal to the level of the card.\n  - Enhance a spell that deals damage, gaining a bonus to your damage roll equal to twice the level of the card."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A whispering orb or a family heirloom",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "What did you do that made the people in your community wary of you?",
        "What mentor taught you to control your untamed magic, and why are they no longer able to guide you?",
        "You have a deep fear you hide from everyone. What is it, and why does it scare you?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "Why do you trust me so deeply?",
        "What did I do that makes you cautious around me?",
        "Why do we keep our shared past a secret?"
      ],
      "description": "Not all innate magic users choose to hone their craft, but those who do can become powerful sorcerers. The gifts of these wielders are passed down through families, even if the family is unaware of or reluctant to practice them. A sorcerer’s abilities can range from the elemental to the illusionary and beyond, and many practitioners band together into collectives based on their talents. The act of becoming a formidable sorcerer is not the practice of acquiring power, but learning to cultivate and control the power one already possesses. The magic of a misguided or undisciplined sorcerer is a dangerous force indeed.\n\nDOMAINS – Arcana & Midnight",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/sorcerer/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warlock",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Warlock",
      "slug": "warlock",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WARLOCK",
        "lineStart": 1730,
        "lineEnd": 1752,
        "pdfPageStart": 26,
        "pdfPageEnd": 26
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/dread"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/grace"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/pact-of-the-endless"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/pact-of-the-wrathful"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 11,
      "startingHitPoints": 5,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Patron’s Boon",
        "rulesText": "When you fail a roll, you can spend 3 Hope to reroll with advantage."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Patron’s Pact",
          "rulesText": "You have committed yourself to a supernatural entity—such as a god, fae, or demon—in exchange for power. Write their name on your character sheet, then work with your GM to determine their sphere of influence (such as Nature, Chaos, Wisdom, Mischief, Love, War, Justice, or Death). Before making an action roll that relates to your patron’s sphere of influence, you can spend a Favor to call upon their aid, rolling your Patron Die and adding its result to the total. Your Patron Die starts at a d6 and increases to a d8 at level 5."
        },
        {
          "name": "Favor",
          "rulesText": "You start with 3 Favor. You can use a downtime move to show tribute to your patron. Describe how and gain Favor equal to your Spellcast trait. Additionally, when you succeed on an action roll with Hope, you can choose to gain a Favor instead of a Hope.\n\n> Note: The maximum Favor you can hold at one time is 6."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A carving that symbolizes your patron or a ring you can’t remove",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Who from your community shunned you after you made a pact with your patron?",
        "What desperate situation led you to pledge your life to your patron?",
        "Your patron has given you one task you must accomplish above all else. What is it, and why does it worry you?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "Why do you think I confide in you about what my patron says and does?",
        "Our relationship has changed since you saw me show tribute to my patron. What did you see, and how has it affected you?",
        "I once did something very foolish, and you’ve never let me live it down. What was it?"
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "name": "Sphere of Influence Examples",
          "rulesText": "When building a warlock, choose their patron’s sphere of influence from the list below or work with the GM to make your own.\n\n| | | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ambition | Leaders | Secrets |\n| Artists | Love | Soldiers |\n| Chaos | Mercy | Strength |\n| Darkness | Mischief | Travelers |\n| Death | Nature | Tricksters |\n| Gamblers | Protectors | Truth |\n| Honor | Revenge | War |\n| Justice | Scholars | Wisdom |"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Those who’ve traded their lives—or perhaps even their souls—to an otherworldly patron in exchange for incredible power are known as warlocks. Often, these mortals have reached a point of desperation that leads them to make this sacrifice: they hope to protect themselves or a loved one, aid their community, seek vengeance, increase their status, or otherwise further their ambitions. The powerful entities they entreat are as varied as the warlocks themselves: gods, spirits, demons, or other beings unknown to even the mortal who makes the pact. The entities each have their own sphere of influence that defines their otherworldly power, and those that collect souls are rarely known for their benevolence. Thus, a warlock’s power is defined by the relationship they maintain with their benefactor. Despite their terrifying magic, warlocks might find that someone or something else is pulling their strings.\n\nDOMAINS – Dread & Grace",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/warlock/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warrior",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Warrior",
      "slug": "warrior",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WARRIOR",
        "lineStart": 1845,
        "lineEnd": 1869,
        "pdfPageStart": 28,
        "pdfPageEnd": 28
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/blade"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/bone"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/call-of-the-brave"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/call-of-the-slayer"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 11,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "No Mercy",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to gain a +1 bonus to your attack rolls until your next rest."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Attack of Opportunity",
          "rulesText": "If an adversary within Melee range attempts to leave that range, make a reaction roll using a trait of your choice against their Difficulty. Choose one effect on a success, or two if you critically succeed:\n\n  - They can’t move from where they are.\n  - You deal damage to them equal to your primary weapon’s damage.\n  - You move with them."
        },
        {
          "name": "Combat Training",
          "rulesText": "You ignore burden when equipping weapons. When you deal physical damage, you gain a bonus to your damage roll equal to your level."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "The drawing of a lover or a sharpening stone",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Who taught you to fight, and why did they stay behind when you left home?",
        "Somebody defeated you in battle years ago and left you to die. Who was it, and how did they betray you?",
        "What legendary place have you always wanted to visit, and why is it so special?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "We knew each other long before this party came together. How?",
        "What mundane task do you usually help me with off the battlefield?",
        "What fear am I helping you overcome?"
      ],
      "description": "Becoming a warrior requires years, often a lifetime, of training and dedication to the mastery of weapons and violence. While many who seek to fight hone only their strength, warriors understand the importance of an agile body and mind, making them some of the most sought-after fighters across the realms. Frequently, warriors find employment within an army, a band of mercenaries, or even a royal guard, but their potential is wasted in any position where they cannot continue to improve and expand their skills. Warriors are known to have a favored weapon; to come between them and their blade would be a grievous mistake.\n\nDOMAINS – Blade & Bone",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/warrior/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/witch",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Witch",
      "slug": "witch",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WITCH",
        "lineStart": 1928,
        "lineEnd": 1956,
        "pdfPageStart": 29,
        "pdfPageEnd": 29
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/dread"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/sage"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/hedge"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/moon"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 10,
      "startingHitPoints": 6,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Witch’s Charm",
        "rulesText": "When you or an ally within Far range fails an action roll, you can spend 3 Hope to change it into a success with Fear instead."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Hex",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to temporarily Hex a target within\n\nFar range. While Hexed, the target gains a penalty to their damage rolls and Difficulty equal to your tier. The maximum number of creatures you can Hex at one time is equal to your Spellcast trait."
        },
        {
          "name": "Commune",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest during a moment of calm, you can commune with an ancestor, a deity, a spirit, or an otherworldly being. Ask them a question, then roll a number of d6s equal to your Spellcast trait. Choose one of the results and reference the chart below for the effect.\n\n| Roll | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1–3 | You taste a flavor, smell a scent, or feel a sensation relevant to the answer. |\n| 4–5 | You hear sounds or see a vision relevant to the answer. |\n| 6 | You psychically experience a scene relevant to the answer as if you were there. |"
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A small, harmless pet or a scrying stone",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "Who from your community feared your magical craft? What rumor did they spread about you, and what truth did it contain?",
        "You once used your power to help someone in a dire situation. Who were they, and why did they come to you?",
        "Your magic once opened a door best left closed. Who or what was on the other side?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What unique ritual or practice have I taught you that we now perform together?",
        "I once appeared to you in a dream and shared a vision of the future. What did I tell you?",
        "What do you typically come to me for advice about?"
      ],
      "description": "Witches are magical practitioners who commune with the forces of nature and entities from realms beyond. These spellcasters call forth power through craft, combining the tangible and ephemeral by casting spells, murmuring incantations, creating talismans, weaving illusions, and maintaining other personalized practices. They can protect their allies and harm their enemies by invoking powerful forces beyond themselves—supernatural beings such as ancestors, deities, or aspects of nature. Often, their magical knowledge is passed down from these entities or through many generations of practitioners who gather in small groups known as covens. Witches are frequently feared and misunderstood, as their methods can appear mysterious, strange, and even macabre to the uninitiated. Whichever path they walk, a witch treads the boundary between light and shadow without fear.\n\nDOMAINS – Sage & Dread",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/witch/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/wizard",
      "@type": "CharacterClass",
      "name": "Wizard",
      "slug": "wizard",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WIZARD",
        "lineStart": 2037,
        "lineEnd": 2059,
        "pdfPageStart": 30,
        "pdfPageEnd": 30
      },
      "domains": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/codex"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/domains/splendor"
        }
      ],
      "subclasses": [
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/school-of-knowledge"
        },
        {
          "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/school-of-war"
        }
      ],
      "startingEvasion": 11,
      "startingHitPoints": 5,
      "hopeFeature": {
        "name": "Not This Time",
        "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to force an adversary within Far range to reroll an attack or damage roll."
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Prestidigitation",
          "rulesText": "You can perform harmless, subtle magical effects at will. For example, you can change an object’s color, create a smell, light a candle, cause a tiny object to float, illuminate a room, or repair a small object."
        },
        {
          "name": "Strange Patterns",
          "rulesText": "Choose a number between 1 and 12. When you roll that number on a Duality Die, gain a Hope or clear a Stress.\n\nYou can change this number when you take a long rest."
        }
      ],
      "classItems": "A book you’re trying to translate or a tiny, harmless elemental pet",
      "backgroundQuestions": [
        "What responsibilities did your community once count on you for? How did you let them down?",
        "You’ve spent your life searching for a book or object of great significance. What is it, and why is it so important to you?",
        "You have a powerful rival. Who are they, and why are you so determined to defeat them?"
      ],
      "connections": [
        "What favor have I asked of you that you’re not sure you can fulfill?",
        "What weird hobby or strange fascination do we both share?",
        "What secret about yourself have you entrusted only to me?"
      ],
      "description": "Whether through an institution or individual study, those known as wizards acquire and hone immense magical power over years of learning using a variety of tools, including books, stones, potions, and herbs. Some wizards dedicate their lives to mastering a particular school of magic, while others learn from a wide variety of disciplines. Many wizards become wise and powerful figures in their communities, advising rulers, providing medicines and healing, and even leading war councils. While these mages all work toward the common goal of collecting magical knowledge, wizards often have the most conflict within their own ranks, as the acquisition, keeping, and sharing of powerful secrets is a topic of intense debate that has resulted in innumerable deaths.\n\nDOMAINS – Codex & Splendor",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/classes/wizard/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/beastbound",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Beastbound",
      "slug": "beastbound",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "BEASTBOUND",
        "lineStart": 1308,
        "lineEnd": 1332,
        "pdfPageStart": 20,
        "pdfPageEnd": 20
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/ranger"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Companion",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You have an animal companion of your choice (at the GM’s discretion). They stay by your side unless you tell them otherwise.\n\nTake the Ranger Companion sheet. When you level up your character, choose a level-up option for your companion from this sheet as well."
        },
        {
          "name": "Expert Training",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Choose an additional level-up option for your companion."
        },
        {
          "name": "Battle-Bonded",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When an adversary attacks you while they’re within your companion’s Melee range, you gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against the attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Advanced Training",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Choose two additional level-up options for your companion."
        },
        {
          "name": "Loyal Friend",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, when the damage from an attack would mark your companion’s last Stress or your last Hit Point and you’re within Close range of each other, you or your companion can rush to the other’s side and take that damage instead."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Beastbound if you want to form a deep bond with an animal ally.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Agility",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/beastbound/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/call-of-the-brave",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Call of the Brave",
      "slug": "call-of-the-brave",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "CALL OF THE BRAVE",
        "lineStart": 1875,
        "lineEnd": 1891,
        "pdfPageStart": 28,
        "pdfPageEnd": 28
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warrior"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Courage",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you fail a roll with Fear, you gain a Hope."
        },
        {
          "name": "Battle Ritual",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, before you attempt something incredibly dangerous or face off against a foe who clearly outmatches you, describe what ritual you perform or preparations you make. When you do, clear 2 Stress and gain 2 Hope."
        },
        {
          "name": "Rise to the Challenge",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You are vigilant in the face of mounting danger. While you have 2 or fewer Hit Points unmarked, you can roll a d20 as your Hope Die."
        },
        {
          "name": "Camaraderie",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Your unwavering bravery is a rallying point for your allies. You can initiate a Tag Team Roll one additional time per session. Additionally, when an ally initiates a Tag Team Roll with you, they only need to spend 2 Hope to do so."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Call of the Brave if you want to use the might of your enemies to fuel your own power.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/call-of-the-brave/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/call-of-the-slayer",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Call of the Slayer",
      "slug": "call-of-the-slayer",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "CALL OF THE SLAYER",
        "lineStart": 1893,
        "lineEnd": 1926,
        "pdfPageStart": 28,
        "pdfPageEnd": 29
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warrior"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Slayer",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You gain a pool of dice called Slayer Dice. On a roll with Hope, you can place a d6 on this card instead of gaining a Hope, adding the die to the pool. You can store a number of Slayer Dice equal to your Proficiency. When you make an attack roll or damage roll, you can spend any number of these Slayer Dice, rolling them and adding their result to the roll. At the end of each session, clear any unspent Slayer Dice on this card and gain a Hope per die cleared."
        },
        {
          "name": "Weapon Specialist",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You can wield multiple weapons with dangerous ease. When you succeed on an attack, you can spend a Hope to add one of the damage dice from your secondary weapon to the damage roll. Additionally, once per long rest when you roll your Slayer Dice, reroll any 1s."
        },
        {
          "name": "Martial Preparation",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You’re an inspirational warrior to all who travel with you. Your party gains access to the Martial Preparation downtime move. To use this move during a rest, describe how you instruct and train with your party. You and each ally who chooses this downtime move gain a d6 Slayer Die. A PC with a Slayer Die can spend it to roll the die and add the result to an attack or damage roll of their choice."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Call of the Slayer if you want to strike down adversaries with immense force.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/call-of-the-slayer/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/divine-wielder",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Divine Wielder",
      "slug": "divine-wielder",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DIVINE WIELDER",
        "lineStart": 1564,
        "lineEnd": 1584,
        "pdfPageStart": 24,
        "pdfPageEnd": 24
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/seraph"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Spirit Weapon",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you have an equipped weapon with a range of Melee or Very Close, it can fly from your hand to attack an adversary within Close range and then return to you. You can mark a Stress to target an additional adversary within range with the same attack roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Sparing Touch",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, touch a creature and clear 2 Hit Points or 2 Stress from them."
        },
        {
          "name": "Devout",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you roll your Prayer Dice, you can roll an additional die and discard the lowest result. Additionally, you can use your “Sparing Touch” feature twice instead of once per long rest."
        },
        {
          "name": "Sacred Resonance",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When you roll damage for your “Spirit Weapon” feature, if any of the die results match, double the value of each matching die. For example, if you roll two 5s, they count as two 10s."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Divine Wielder if you want to dominate the battlefield with a legendary weapon.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Strength",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/divine-wielder/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/elemental-origin",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Elemental Origin",
      "slug": "elemental-origin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ELEMENTAL ORIGIN",
        "lineStart": 1660,
        "lineEnd": 1685,
        "pdfPageStart": 25,
        "pdfPageEnd": 25
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/sorcerer"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Elementalist",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Choose one of the following elements at character creation: air, earth, fire, lightning, water.\n\nYou can shape this element into harmless effects. Additionally, spend a Hope and describe how your control over this element helps an action roll you’re about to make, then either gain a +2 bonus to the roll or a +3 bonus to the roll’s damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Natural Evasion",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You can call forth your element to protect you from harm. When an attack roll against you succeeds, you can mark a Stress and describe how you use your element to defend you. When you do, roll a d6 and add its result to your Evasion against the attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Transcendence",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can transform into a physical manifestation of your element. When you do, describe your transformation and choose two of the following benefits to gain until your next rest:\n\n  - +4 bonus to your Severe threshold\n  - +1 bonus to a character trait of your choice\n  - +1 bonus to your Proficiency\n  - +2 bonus to your Evasion"
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Elemental Origin if you want to channel raw magic to take the shape of a particular element.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Instinct",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/elemental-origin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/executioners-guild",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Executioners Guild",
      "slug": "executioners-guild",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "EXECUTIONERS GUILD",
        "lineStart": 402,
        "lineEnd": 426,
        "pdfPageStart": 9,
        "pdfPageEnd": 9
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/assassin"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "First Strike",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "The first time in a scene you succeed on an attack, you deal double damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ambush",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Your “Marked for Death” feature uses d6s instead of d4s."
        },
        {
          "name": "Death Strike",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you deal Severe damage to a creature, you can mark a Stress to force them to mark an additional Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Scorpion’s Poise",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against attacks made by a creature you’ve Marked for Death."
        },
        {
          "name": "True Strike",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest when you fail an attack, you can spend a Hope to make it a success instead."
        },
        {
          "name": "Backstab",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Your “Marked for Death” feature uses d8s instead of d6s."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Executioners Guild if you want to strike down your targets with lethal precision.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Agility",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/executioners-guild/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/hedge",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Hedge",
      "slug": "hedge",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "HEDGE",
        "lineStart": 1962,
        "lineEnd": 1984,
        "pdfPageStart": 29,
        "pdfPageEnd": 29
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/witch"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Herbal Remedies",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you or an ally in the scene clears 1 or more Hit Points or Stress as the result of using a consumable, increase the number cleared by 1."
        },
        {
          "name": "Enchanted Talisman",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, you can imbue a small item with your protective essence. Spend any number of Hope to place an equal number of tokens on this card. When the person holding the talisman takes damage, spend a token to reduce the number of Hit Points they mark by one. Clear all tokens from this card when you take a rest."
        },
        {
          "name": "Walk Between Worlds",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "During a moment of calm, make a Spellcast Roll (13). Once per rest on a success, you can mark a Stress to step beyond the veil of death and converse with any nearby spirits. Place a number of tokens equal to your Spellcast trait on this card and remove one each time a spirit answers a question. When the last token is removed or at the end of the scene, you return to the Mortal Realm in the same spot you left it."
        },
        {
          "name": "Vexing Malison",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You have advantage on attacks against Hexed creatures."
        },
        {
          "name": "Circle of Power",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, mark a circle on the ground outlining a Very Close area around you, and place a number of tokens equal to your Spellcast trait on this card. While within this circle, you and your allies gain a +2 bonus to damage thresholds, attack rolls, and Evasion. Remove a token each time you or an ally within the circle makes an action roll or evades an attack. This spell lasts until the last token is removed or you exit the circle."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Hedge if you want to use your craft to empower yourself and your party.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Knowledge",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/hedge/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/juggernaut",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Juggernaut",
      "slug": "juggernaut",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "JUGGERNAUT",
        "lineStart": 605,
        "lineEnd": 625,
        "pdfPageStart": 12,
        "pdfPageEnd": 12
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/brawler"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Rugged",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +3 bonus to your Severe damage threshold."
        },
        {
          "name": "Overwhelm",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an attack against a target, you can spend a Hope to throw the target within Close range or to force them to mark a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Surrounded",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you make an attack with a Melee weapon, you can spend any number of Hope to target an equal number of additional creatures within Melee range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Eye for an Eye",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest when an adversary within Melee range forces you to mark any number of Hit Points, you can mark a Stress to force them to mark the same number of Hit Points."
        },
        {
          "name": "Pummeljoy",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When you critically succeed on a Melee weapon attack, you gain an additional Hope, clear an additional Stress, and gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for that attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Not Done Yet",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When you take Severe damage, you can gain a Hope or clear a Stress."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Juggernaut if you want to pulverize your opponents with crushing blows.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/juggernaut/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/martial-artist",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Martial Artist",
      "slug": "martial-artist",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "MARTIAL ARTIST",
        "lineStart": 627,
        "lineEnd": 663,
        "pdfPageStart": 12,
        "pdfPageEnd": 12
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/brawler"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Stance Fighter",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You can channel your inner resolve to shift into martial stances that grant you special benefits in combat.\n\nTake the Martial Stances sheet and choose two martial stances from Tier 1. Each time you level up your character, choose an additional stance from your tier or lower."
        },
        {
          "name": "Keen Defenses",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you’re targeted by an attack, you can spend a Focus to gain a bonus to your Evasion equal to your tier against the attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Focus Cannon",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Focus to make an Instinct Roll against an adversary within Far range. On a success, deal d20+3 magic damage using your Proficiency."
        },
        {
          "name": "Limit Breaker",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, you can perform an unbelievable feat of athletic prowess, such as running across water, leaping between distant rooftops, or scaling a building without needing to roll. When you do, gain a Hope and clear a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Flow State",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You can mark a Stress instead of spending a Focus to shift into a different stance. Additionally, you can spend a Focus instead of marking a Stress to start a combo strike."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Martial Artist if you want to use a variety of fighting styles to eliminate your foes.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/martial-artist/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/moon",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Moon",
      "slug": "moon",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "MOON",
        "lineStart": 1988,
        "lineEnd": 2035,
        "pdfPageStart": 30,
        "pdfPageEnd": 30
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/witch"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Night’s Glamour",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Make a Spellcast Roll (13) to Glamour yourself in a bewitching facade. While Glamoured, you have advantage on rolls that leverage your illusory appearance. Additionally, adversaries within Close range must mark a Stress to attack you.\n\nUnless you mark a Stress to maintain your Glamour, it drops when you mark a Hit Point or deal damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Moonbeam",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per session, you can conjure a column of moonlight that illuminates the area within Close range until the end of the scene. While bathed in this moonlight, you and your allies gain a +1 bonus to Spellcast Rolls and can see through illusions."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ire of Pale Light",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When a Hexed creature within Very Far range fails an attack, they must mark a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Lunar Phases",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "At the beginning of each session, roll a d6 and place it on this card. You gain the matching effect until the end of session.\n\n| Roll | Phase | Effect |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | New | Spend a Hope to negate Minor damage. |\n| 2–3 | Waxing | +2 to damage rolls |\n| 4 | Full | +3 to damage thresholds |\n| 5–6 | Waning | +1 to Evasion |\n\nOnce per rest, you can spend a Hope to increase the value of this die by one. If you increase the value of a 6, it becomes a 1."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Moon if you want to embody celestial power to amplify your magic.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Instinct",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/moon/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/nightwalker",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Nightwalker",
      "slug": "nightwalker",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "NIGHTWALKER",
        "lineStart": 1465,
        "lineEnd": 1487,
        "pdfPageStart": 23,
        "pdfPageEnd": 23
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/rogue"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Shadow Stepper",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You can move from shadow to shadow. When you move into an area of darkness or a shadow cast by another creature or object, you can mark a Stress to disappear from where you are and reappear inside another shadow within Far range. When you reappear, you are Cloaked."
        },
        {
          "name": "Dark Cloud",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Make a Spellcast Roll (15). On a success, create a temporary dark cloud that covers any area within Close range. Anyone in this cloud can’t see outside of it, and anyone outside of it can’t see in. You’re considered Cloaked from any adversary for whom the cloud blocks line of sight."
        },
        {
          "name": "Adrenaline",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "While you're Vulnerable, add your level to your damage rolls."
        },
        {
          "name": "Fleeting Shadow",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your Evasion. You can use your “Shadow Stepper” feature to move within Very Far range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Vanishing Act",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to become Cloaked at any time. When Cloaked from this feature, you automatically clear the Restrained condition if you have it. You remain Cloaked in this way until you roll with Fear or until your next rest."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Nightwalker if you want to manipulate shadows to maneuver through the environment.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Finesse",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/nightwalker/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/pact-of-the-endless",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Pact of the Endless",
      "slug": "pact-of-the-endless",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "PACT OF THE ENDLESS",
        "lineStart": 1775,
        "lineEnd": 1799,
        "pdfPageStart": 27,
        "pdfPageEnd": 27
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warlock"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Patron’s Mantle",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Favor to cloak yourself in a terrifying aspect of your Patron that lasts until you take Severe damage or the scene ends. While this effect is active, you gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your tier and have advantage on action rolls to intimidate a target."
        },
        {
          "name": "Deathless Embrace",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, spend any number of Favor to roll an equal number of Patron Dice. For each result of 4 or higher, clear a Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Harrowing Invocation",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When an adversary targets you or an ally within Very Close range with an attack, you can spend a Favor to give them disadvantage on the roll. If the adversary fails the roll, they must also mark a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Damage Sink",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, you can spend a Favor to halve incoming damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Dark Aegis",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest when you would take damage, you can spend a Favor instead of marking Hit Points."
        },
        {
          "name": "Draining Bane",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When an adversary targets you or an ally within Very Close range with an attack, you can spend a Favor to Drain them. When you do, they must mark a Stress, and you can clear a Stress. While Drained, the target uses a d12 instead of a d20 for attack rolls (including for advantage or disadvantage) until they fail a roll."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Pact of the Endless if you want to stand strong against enemies and avoid death.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Presence",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/pact-of-the-endless/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/pact-of-the-wrathful",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Pact of the Wrathful",
      "slug": "pact-of-the-wrathful",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "PACT OF THE WRATHFUL",
        "lineStart": 1801,
        "lineEnd": 1841,
        "pdfPageStart": 27,
        "pdfPageEnd": 27
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/warlock"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Patron’s Fury",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Favor to imbue your attacks with your Patron’s power until you deal Severe damage or the scene ends. When you roll damage while this effect is active, you also roll a number of Patron Dice equal to your tier and add their total to the damage dealt."
        },
        {
          "name": "Deadly Vengeance",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you mark any number of Hit Points from an attack, you can spend a Favor to roll an equal number of Patron Dice. For each result of 4 or higher, the attacker marks a Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Menacing Reach",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Favor to increase the range of your primary weapon by one step (such as Melee to Very Close or Very Close to Close) to a maximum of Very Far range. This effect ends when you make a successful attack with that weapon."
        },
        {
          "name": "Diminish My Foes",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed with Hope on an action roll against a target, you can spend any number of Favor to force the target to mark an equal number of Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Fearsome Attack",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Spend a Favor to reroll any number of your damage dice. You can continue spending Favor to use this feature on the same damage roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Otherworldly Ire",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest when you take damage, you can spend any number of Favor to roll that many Patron Dice and target a number of creatures within Close range equal to the highest result. Each target must mark a Hit Point."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Pact of the Wrathful if you want to destroy those who act against you.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Presence",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/pact-of-the-wrathful/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/poisoners-guild",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Poisoners Guild",
      "slug": "poisoners-guild",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "POISONERS GUILD",
        "lineStart": 430,
        "lineEnd": 481,
        "pdfPageStart": 10,
        "pdfPageEnd": 10
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/assassin"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Toxic Concoctions",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to place 1d4+1 tokens on this card. When you make a successful weapon attack, you can spend a token to afflict the target with a poison. You know these poisons:\n\n  - Ghost Petal: The target becomes temporarily Vulnerable.\n  - Grave Spore: The target must also mark a Stress.\n  - Leech Weed: You deal an extra 1d6 damage on this attack.\n\nWhen you take a long rest, clear all unspent tokens."
        },
        {
          "name": "Poison Compendium",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You also know these poisons:\n\n  - Midnight Vine: The target has disadvantage on attack rolls until it marks a Stress to clear this condition.\n  - Gorgon Root: The target becomes temporarily Restrained."
        },
        {
          "name": "Twin Fang",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When you afflict a target Marked for Death with a poison you know, you can spend an additional token to also inflict the effect of a second poison you know."
        },
        {
          "name": "Venomancer",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You also know these poisons:\n\n  - Blight Seed: The target gains a −3 penalty to their damage thresholds until the end of the scene. This effect can’t stack.\n  - Fear Leaf: You deal extra damage equal to the result of your Fear Die on this attack.\n  - Corpse Thorn: The target gains disadvantage on reaction rolls until the end of the scene."
        },
        {
          "name": "Adder’s Blessing",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You are immune to poisons and other toxins."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Poisoners Guild if you want to debilitate your targets with punishing afflictions.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Knowledge",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/poisoners-guild/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/primal-origin",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Primal Origin",
      "slug": "primal-origin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "PRIMAL ORIGIN",
        "lineStart": 1687,
        "lineEnd": 1728,
        "pdfPageStart": 25,
        "pdfPageEnd": 26
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/sorcerer"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Manipulate Magic",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Your primal origin allows you to modify the essence of magic itself. After you cast a spell or make an attack using a weapon that deals magic damage, you can mark a Stress to do one of the following:\n\n  - Extend the spell or attack’s reach by one range\n  - Gain a +2 bonus to the action roll’s result\n  - Double a damage die of your choice\n  - Hit an additional target within range"
        },
        {
          "name": "Enchanted Aid",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You can enhance the magic of others with your essence. When you Help an Ally with a Spellcast Roll, you can roll a d8 as your advantage die. Once per long rest, after an ally has made a Spellcast Roll with your help, you can swap the results of their Duality Dice."
        },
        {
          "name": "Arcane Charge",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You can gather magical energy to enhance your capabilities. When you take magic damage, you become Charged. Alternatively, you can spend 2 Hope to become Charged. When you successfully make an attack that deals magic damage while Charged, you can clear your Charge to either gain a +10 bonus to the damage roll or gain a +3 bonus to the Difficulty of a reaction roll the spell causes the target to make. You stop being Charged at your next long rest."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Primal Origin if you want to extend the versatility of your spells in powerful ways.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Instinct",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/primal-origin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/school-of-knowledge",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "School of Knowledge",
      "slug": "school-of-knowledge",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SCHOOL OF KNOWLEDGE",
        "lineStart": 2067,
        "lineEnd": 2091,
        "pdfPageStart": 31,
        "pdfPageEnd": 31
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/wizard"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Prepared",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Take an additional domain card of your level or lower from a domain you have access to."
        },
        {
          "name": "Adept",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you Utilize an Experience, you can mark a Stress instead of spending a Hope. If you do, double your Experience modifier for that roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Accomplished",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Take an additional domain card of your level or lower from a domain you have access to."
        },
        {
          "name": "Perfect Recall",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, when you recall a domain card in your vault, you can reduce its Recall Cost by 1."
        },
        {
          "name": "Brilliant",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Take an additional domain card of your level or lower from a domain you have access to."
        },
        {
          "name": "Honed Expertise",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When you use an Experience, roll a d6. On a result of 5 or higher, you can use it without spending Hope."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the School of Knowledge if you want a keen understanding of the world around you.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Knowledge",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/school-of-knowledge/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/school-of-war",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "School of War",
      "slug": "school-of-war",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SCHOOL OF WAR",
        "lineStart": 2093,
        "lineEnd": 2133,
        "pdfPageStart": 31,
        "pdfPageEnd": 31
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/wizard"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Battlemage",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You’ve focused your studies on becoming an unconquerable force on the battlefield. Gain an additional Hit Point slot."
        },
        {
          "name": "Face Your Fear",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed with Fear on an attack roll, you deal an extra 1d10 magic damage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Conjure Shield",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You can maintain a protective barrier of magic. While you have at least 2 Hope, you add your Proficiency to your Evasion."
        },
        {
          "name": "Fueled by Fear",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "The extra magic damage from your “Face Your Fear” feature increases to 2d10."
        },
        {
          "name": "Thrive in Chaos",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an attack, you can mark a Stress after rolling damage to force the target to mark an additional Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Have No Fear",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "The extra magic damage from your “Face Your Fear” feature increases to 3d10."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the School of War if you want to utilize trained magic for violence.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Knowledge",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/school-of-war/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/stalwart",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Stalwart",
      "slug": "stalwart",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "STALWART",
        "lineStart": 1222,
        "lineEnd": 1242,
        "pdfPageStart": 19,
        "pdfPageEnd": 19
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/guardian"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Unwavering",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Iron Will",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you take physical damage, you can mark an additional Armor Slot to reduce the severity."
        },
        {
          "name": "Unrelenting",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +2 bonus to your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Partners-in-Arms",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When an ally within Very Close range takes damage, you can mark an Armor Slot to reduce the severity by one threshold."
        },
        {
          "name": "Undaunted",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +3 bonus to your damage thresholds."
        },
        {
          "name": "Loyal Protector",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "When an ally within Close range has 2 or fewer Hit Points and would take damage, you can mark a Stress to sprint to their side and take the damage instead."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Stalwart if you want to take heavy blows and keep fighting.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/stalwart/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/syndicate",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Syndicate",
      "slug": "syndicate",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SYNDICATE",
        "lineStart": 1489,
        "lineEnd": 1536,
        "pdfPageStart": 23,
        "pdfPageEnd": 23
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/rogue"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Well-Connected",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you arrive in a prominent town or environment, you know somebody who calls this place home. Give them a name, note how you think they could be useful, and choose one fact from the following list:\n\n  - They owe me a favor, but they’ll be hard to find.\n  - They’re going to ask for something in exchange.\n  - They’re always in a great deal of trouble.\n  - We used to be together. It’s a long story.\n  - We didn’t part on great terms."
        },
        {
          "name": "Contacts Everywhere",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per session, you can briefly call on a shady contact. Choose one of the following benefits and describe what brought them here to help you in this moment:\n\n  - They provide 1 handful of gold, a unique tool, or a mundane object that the situation requires.\n  - On your next action roll, their help provides a +3 bonus to the result of your Hope or Fear Die.\n  - The next time you deal damage, they snipe from the shadows, adding 2d8 to your damage roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Reliable Backup",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You can use your “Contacts Everywhere” feature three times per session. The following options are added to the list of benefits you can choose from when you use that feature:\n\n  - When you mark 1 or more Hit Points, they can rush out to shield you, reducing the Hit Points marked by 1.\n  - When you make a Presence Roll in conversation, they back you up. You can roll a d20 as your Hope Die."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Syndicate if you want to have a web of contacts everywhere you go.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Finesse",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/syndicate/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/troubadour",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Troubadour",
      "slug": "troubadour",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "TROUBADOUR",
        "lineStart": 509,
        "lineEnd": 531,
        "pdfPageStart": 11,
        "pdfPageEnd": 11
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/bard"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Gifted Performer",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You can play three different types of songs, once each per long rest; describe how you perform for others to gain the listed benefit:\n\n  - Relaxing Song: You and all allies within Close range clear a Hit Point.\n  - Epic Song: Make a target within Close range temporarily Vulnerable.\n  - Heartbreaking Song: You and all allies within Close range gain a Hope."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Troubadour if you want to play music to bolster your allies.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Presence",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/troubadour/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/vengeance",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Vengeance",
      "slug": "vengeance",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "VENGEANCE",
        "lineStart": 1244,
        "lineEnd": 1278,
        "pdfPageStart": 19,
        "pdfPageEnd": 20
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/guardian"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "At Ease",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Gain an additional Stress slot."
        },
        {
          "name": "Revenge",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When an adversary within Melee range succeeds on an attack against you, you can mark 2 Stress to force the attacker to mark a Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Act of Reprisal",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When an adversary damages an ally within Melee range, you gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for the next successful attack you make against that adversary."
        },
        {
          "name": "Nemesis",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Spend 2 Hope to Prioritize an adversary until your next rest. When you make an attack against your Prioritized adversary, you can swap the results of your Hope and Fear Dice. You can only Prioritize one adversary at a time."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Vengeance if you want to strike down enemies who harm you or your allies.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/vengeance/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/warden-of-renewal",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Warden of Renewal",
      "slug": "warden-of-renewal",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WARDEN OF RENEWAL",
        "lineStart": 800,
        "lineEnd": 838,
        "pdfPageStart": 15,
        "pdfPageEnd": 15
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/druid"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Clarity of Nature",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can create a space of natural serenity within Close range. When you spend a few minutes resting within the space, clear Stress equal to your Instinct, distributed as you choose between you and your allies."
        },
        {
          "name": "Regeneration",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Touch a creature and spend 3 Hope. That creature clears 1d4 Hit Points."
        },
        {
          "name": "Regenerative Reach",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "You can target creatures within Very Close range with your “Regeneration” feature."
        },
        {
          "name": "Warden’s Protection",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, spend 2 Hope to clear 2 Hit Points on 1d4 allies within Close range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Defender",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Your animal transformation embodies a healing guardian spirit. When you’re in Beastform and an ally within Close range marks 2 or more Hit Points, you can mark a Stress to reduce the number of Hit Points they mark by 1."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Warden of Renewal if you want to use powerful magic to heal your party.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Instinct",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/warden-of-renewal/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/warden-of-the-elements",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Warden of the Elements",
      "slug": "warden-of-the-elements",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WARDEN OF THE ELEMENTS",
        "lineStart": 763,
        "lineEnd": 796,
        "pdfPageStart": 14,
        "pdfPageEnd": 14
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/druid"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Elemental Incarnation",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to Channel one of the following elements until you take Severe damage or until your next rest:\n\n  - Fire: When an adversary within Melee range deals damage to you, they take 1d10 magic damage.\n  - Earth: Gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your Proficiency.\n  - Water: When you deal damage to an adversary within Melee range, all other adversaries within Very Close range must mark a Stress.\n  - Air: You can hover, gaining advantage on Agility Rolls."
        },
        {
          "name": "Elemental Aura",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest while Channeling, you can assume an aura matching your element. The aura affects targets within Close range until your Channeling ends.\n\n  - Fire: When an adversary marks 1 or more Hit Points, they must also mark a Stress.\n  - Earth: Your allies gain a +1 bonus to Strength.\n  - Water: When an adversary deals damage to you, you can mark a Stress to move them anywhere within Very Close range of where they are.\n  - Air: When you or an ally takes damage from an attack beyond Melee range, reduce the damage by 1d8."
        },
        {
          "name": "Elemental Dominion",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "You further embody your element. While Channeling, you gain the following benefit:\n\n  - Fire: You gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency for attacks and spells that deal damage.\n  - Earth: When you would mark Hit Points, roll a d6 per Hit Point marked. For each result of 6, reduce the number of Hit Points you mark by 1.\n  - Water: When an attack against you succeeds, you can mark a Stress to make the attacker temporarily Vulnerable.\n  - Air: You gain a +1 bonus to your Evasion and can fly."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Warden of the Elements if you want to embody the natural elements of the wild.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Instinct",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/warden-of-the-elements/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/wayfinder",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Wayfinder",
      "slug": "wayfinder",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WAYFINDER",
        "lineStart": 1336,
        "lineEnd": 1372,
        "pdfPageStart": 21,
        "pdfPageEnd": 21
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/ranger"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Ruthless Predator",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you make a damage roll, you can mark a Stress to gain a +1 bonus to your Proficiency. Additionally, when you deal Severe damage to an adversary, they must mark a Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Path Forward",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "When you’re traveling to a place you’ve previously visited or you carry an object that has been at the location before, you can identify the shortest, most direct path to your destination."
        },
        {
          "name": "Elusive Predator",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "When your Focus makes an attack against you, you gain a +2 bonus to your Evasion against the attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Apex Predator",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Before you make an attack roll against your Focus, you can spend a Hope. On a successful attack, you remove a Fear from the GM’s Fear pool."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Wayfinder if you want to hunt your prey and strike with deadly force.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Agility",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/wayfinder/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/winged-sentinel",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Winged Sentinel",
      "slug": "winged-sentinel",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WINGED SENTINEL",
        "lineStart": 1586,
        "lineEnd": 1627,
        "pdfPageStart": 24,
        "pdfPageEnd": 25
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/seraph"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Wings of Light",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "You can fly. While flying, you can do the following:\n\n- Mark a Stress to pick up and carry another willing creature approximately your size or smaller.\n- Spend a Hope to deal an extra 1d8 damage on a successful attack."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ethereal Visage",
          "stage": "specialization",
          "rulesText": "Your supernatural visage strikes awe and fear. While flying, you have advantage on Presence Rolls. When you succeed with Hope on a Presence Roll, you can remove a Fear from the GM’s Fear pool instead of gaining Hope."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ascendant",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +4 bonus to your Severe damage threshold."
        },
        {
          "name": "Power of the Gods",
          "stage": "mastery",
          "rulesText": "While flying, you deal an extra 1d12 damage instead of 1d8 from your “Wings of Light” feature."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Winged Sentinel if you want to take flight and strike crushing blows from the sky.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Strength",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/winged-sentinel/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/subclasses/wordsmith",
      "@type": "Subclass",
      "name": "Wordsmith",
      "slug": "wordsmith",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WORDSMITH",
        "lineStart": 533,
        "lineEnd": 573,
        "pdfPageStart": 11,
        "pdfPageEnd": 11
      },
      "parentClass": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/classes/bard"
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Rousing Speech",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can give a heartfelt, inspiring speech. All allies within Far range clear 2 Stress."
        },
        {
          "name": "Heart of a Poet",
          "stage": "foundation",
          "rulesText": "After you make an action roll to impress, persuade, or offend someone, you can spend a Hope to add a d4 to the roll."
        }
      ],
      "description": "Play the Wordsmith if you want to use clever wordplay and captivate crowds.",
      "spellcastTrait": "Presence",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/subclasses/wordsmith/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/aetheris",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Aetheris",
      "slug": "aetheris",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "AETHERIS",
        "lineStart": 2153,
        "lineEnd": 2163,
        "pdfPageStart": 32,
        "pdfPageEnd": 32
      },
      "description": "Aetheris are humanoids most easily recognized by their wings and sacred markings. They are the descendants of angels from the Hallows Above. Aetheris are typically 5 to 7 feet tall, and some may have elongated limbs. They often have vibrantly colored eyes, hair, and skin, though their coloring can include earth tones and pastels. Many members of this ancestry are born with sacred markings—such as glowing sigils or intricate patterns of lines and symbols—but all aetheris develop them as they mature. These symbols change as they age, often because of prophecies, revelations, and divine guidance from the Hallows Above.\n\nAll aetheris have a “hallowed aura” that can manifest voluntarily, such as when they invoke their power, or involuntarily, such as when they feel elation or other strong emotions. When they call forth this radiance, their eyes and bodies might glow, their markings might become more prominent or shift across their skin, or they might have a halo appear atop or around their head. These halos are as unique as a fingerprint and may share similarities with other family members. Perhaps due to their angelic lineage, aetheris usually live for 350 years, and they retain a youthful glow even as they age.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Hallowed Aura",
          "rulesText": "Once per long rest when an ally within Close range rolls with Fear, you can change it into a roll with Hope instead."
        },
        {
          "name": "Celestial Wings",
          "rulesText": "You have wings that allow you to fly. Once per scene while flying, you can spend a Hope instead of marking an Armor Slot."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/aetheris/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/clank",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Clank",
      "slug": "clank",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "CLANK",
        "lineStart": 2165,
        "lineEnd": 2175,
        "pdfPageStart": 32,
        "pdfPageEnd": 32
      },
      "description": "Clanks are sentient mechanical beings built from a variety of materials, including metal, wood, and stone. They can resemble humanoids, animals, or even inanimate objects. Like organic beings, their bodies come in a wide array of sizes. Because of their bespoke construction, many clanks have highly specialized physical configurations. Examples include clawed hands for grasping, wheels for movement, or built-in weaponry.\n\nMany clanks embrace body modifications for style as well as function, and members of other ancestries often turn to clank artisans to construct customized mobility aids and physical adornments. Other ancestries can create clanks, even using their own physical characteristics as inspiration, but it’s also common for clanks to build one another. A clank’s lifespan extends as long as they’re able to acquire or craft new parts, making their physical form effectively immortal. That said, their minds are subject to the effects of time, and deteriorate as the magic that powers them loses potency.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Purposeful Design",
          "rulesText": "Decide who made you and for what purpose. At character creation, choose one of your Experiences that best aligns with this purpose and gain a permanent +1 bonus to it."
        },
        {
          "name": "Efficient",
          "rulesText": "When you take a short rest, you can choose a long rest move instead of a short rest move."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/clank/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/drakona",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Drakona",
      "slug": "drakona",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DRAKONA",
        "lineStart": 2177,
        "lineEnd": 2187,
        "pdfPageStart": 32,
        "pdfPageEnd": 33
      },
      "description": "Drakona resemble wingless dragons in humanoid form and possess a powerful elemental breath. All drakona have thick scales that provide excellent natural armor against both attacks and the forces of nature. They are large in size, ranging from 5 feet to 7 feet on average, with long sharp teeth. New teeth grow throughout a Drakona’s approximately 350-year lifespan, so they are never in danger of permanently losing an incisor. Unlike their dragon ancestors, drakona don’t have wings and can’t fly without magical aid. Members of this ancestry pass down the element of their breath through generations, though in rare cases, a drakona’s elemental power will differ from the rest of their family’s.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Scales",
          "rulesText": "Your scales act as natural protection. When you would take Severe damage, you can mark a Stress to mark 1 fewer Hit Points."
        },
        {
          "name": "Elemental Breath",
          "rulesText": "Choose an element for your breath (such as electricity, fire, or ice). You can use this breath against a target or group of targets within Very Close range, treating it as an Instinct weapon that deals d8 magic damage using your Proficiency."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/drakona/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/dwarf",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Dwarf",
      "slug": "dwarf",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DWARF",
        "lineStart": 2189,
        "lineEnd": 2197,
        "pdfPageStart": 33,
        "pdfPageEnd": 33
      },
      "description": "Dwarves are most easily recognized as short humanoids with square frames, dense musculature, and thick hair. Their average height ranges from 4 to 5 ½ feet, and they are often broad in proportion to their stature. Their skin and nails contain a high amount of keratin, making them naturally resilient. This allows dwarves to embed gemstones into their bodies and decorate themselves with tattoos or piercings. Their hair grows thickly—usually on their heads, but some dwarves have thick hair across their bodies as well. Dwarves of all genders can grow facial hair, which they often style in elaborate arrangements. Typically, dwarves live up to 250 years of age, maintaining their muscle mass well into later life.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Thick Skin",
          "rulesText": "When you take Minor damage, you can mark 2 Stress instead of marking a Hit Point."
        },
        {
          "name": "Increased Fortitude",
          "rulesText": "Spend 3 Hope to halve incoming physical damage."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/dwarf/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/earthkin",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Earthkin",
      "slug": "earthkin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "EARTHKIN",
        "lineStart": 2209,
        "lineEnd": 2219,
        "pdfPageStart": 33,
        "pdfPageEnd": 33
      },
      "description": "Earthkin are humanoids whose bodies are made of flesh and earth. Descended from earth elementals, members of this ancestry are formed of flesh and rock, crystal, clay, soil, sand, or a mixture of these materials. Some are primarily flesh and blood with just one earthen feature, such as skin the color or texture of stone or crystal formations that adorn their heads like hair. Others are a combination, often asymmetrical unions of muscle and natural minerals. Still others are almost entirely soil and stone. Earthkin might choose to mold and decorate the mineral portions of their form by carving designs, embedding gems or metals, or even planting seeds that bloom into adorning foliage. Their coloring comes from their elemental lineage, as even those who are more flesh than earth come in hues reminiscent of marble, gemstones, sand, soil, and more.\n\nThese kin begin their lives as small lumps of earth, such as a boulder, a ball of clay, or a crystal cluster. Though they’ll develop their more humanoid features fairly quickly, earthkin age slowly for most of their approximately 300-year lifespan. Their average height at adulthood ranges from 5 to 6½ feet tall, and the shape of their body is strongly influenced by the primary material it’s composed of. Many earthkin’s injuries appear like fissures in stone; even long after the initial damage has healed, these cracks continue to slowly repair. When they die, earthkin eventually crumble into piles of earth.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Stoneskin",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your Armor Score and damage thresholds at character creation."
        },
        {
          "name": "Immovable",
          "rulesText": "While you’re touching the ground, you can’t be lifted or moved against your will."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/earthkin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/elf",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Elf",
      "slug": "elf",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ELF",
        "lineStart": 2261,
        "lineEnd": 2271,
        "pdfPageStart": 34,
        "pdfPageEnd": 34
      },
      "description": "Elves are typically tall humanoids with pointed ears and acutely attuned senses. Their ears vary in size and pointed shape, and as they age, the tips begin to droop. While elves come in a wide range of body types, they are all fairly tall, with heights ranging from about 6 to 6 ½ feet. All elves have the ability to drop into a celestial trance, rather than sleep. This allows them to rest effectively in a short amount of time.\n\nSome elves possess what is known as a “mystic form,” which occurs when an elf has dedicated themself to the study or protection of the natural world so deeply that their physical form changes. These characteristics can include celestial freckles, the presence of leaves, vines, or flowers in their hair, eyes that flicker like fire, and more. Sometimes these traits are inherited from parents, but if an elf changes their environment or magical focus, their appearance changes over time. Because elves live for about 350 years, these traits can shift more than once throughout their lifespan.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Quick Reactions",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to gain advantage on a reaction roll."
        },
        {
          "name": "Celestial Trance",
          "rulesText": "During a rest, you can drop into a trance to choose an additional downtime move."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/elf/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/emberkin",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Emberkin",
      "slug": "emberkin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "EMBERKIN",
        "lineStart": 2221,
        "lineEnd": 2235,
        "pdfPageStart": 33,
        "pdfPageEnd": 34
      },
      "description": "Emberkin are humanoids whose bodies are made of flesh and fire. Descended from fire elementals, emberkin might manifest their fiery heritage through flaming hair, eyes like embers, luminous streams that dance across their skin, a smoking exterior, or a glow flickering through their muscles, veins, organs, or fingertips. All these features are known to shift or flare with their emotions, and some individuals’ characteristics might be more changeable than another’s. The coloring of their skin and hair resembles the hues of fire: bright red, orange, yellow, charcoal, or even hints of blue. Their ears curve and point like a tongue of fire, and they tend to be warmer in temperature, either purposefully or innately, with some even being hot to the touch.\n\nInfant emberkin resemble small balls of fire or cinders, and because they lack the skill to control their temperature, they often need to be placed in fireproof containment such as dragon eggs, hearths, and cookware. Their humanoid\n\ncharacteristics develop throughout adolescence until they solidify into their adult form. Emberkin range from 5 to 7 feet tall and live about 250 years, their light and heat dimming as they age. When they pass, they blaze brightly for a moment, then burn to ash.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Fireproof",
          "rulesText": "You are immune to damage from magical or mundane flame."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ignition",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to wreathe your primary weapon in flame until the end of the scene. While the weapon is ablaze, it gives off a bright light, and you gain a 1d6 bonus to damage rolls with that weapon."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/emberkin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/faerie",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Faerie",
      "slug": "faerie",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FAERIE",
        "lineStart": 2273,
        "lineEnd": 2279,
        "pdfPageStart": 34,
        "pdfPageEnd": 35
      },
      "description": "Faeries are winged humanoid creatures with insectile features. These characteristics cover a broad spectrum from humanoid to insectoid—some possess additional arms, compound eyes, lantern organs, chitinous exoskeletons, or stingers. Because of their close ties to the natural world, they also frequently possess attributes that allow them to blend in with various plants. The average height of a faerie ranges from about 2 feet to 5 feet, but some faeries grow up to 7 feet tall. All faeries possess membranous wings and they each go through\n\na process of metamorphosis. The process and changes differ from faerie to faerie, but during this transformation each individual manifests the unique appearance they will carry throughout the rest of their approximately 50-year lifespan.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/faerie/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/faun",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Faun",
      "slug": "faun",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FAUN",
        "lineStart": 2287,
        "lineEnd": 2295,
        "pdfPageStart": 35,
        "pdfPageEnd": 35
      },
      "description": "Fauns resemble humanoid goats with curving horns, square pupils, and cloven hooves. Though their appearances may vary, most fauns have a humanoid torso and a goatlike lower body covered in dense fur. Faun faces can be more caprine or more humanlike, and they have a wide variety of ear and horn shapes. Faun horns range from short with minimal curvature to much larger with a distinct curl. The average faun ranges from 4 feet to 6 ½ feet tall, but their height can change dramatically from one moment to the next based on their stance. The majority of fauns have proportionately long limbs, no matter their size or shape, and are known for their ability to deliver powerful blows with their split hooves. Fauns live for roughly 225 years, and as they age, their appearance can become increasingly goatlike.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Caprine Leap",
          "rulesText": "You can leap anywhere within Close range as though you were using normal movement, allowing you to vault obstacles, jump across gaps, or scale barriers with ease."
        },
        {
          "name": "Kick",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can mark a Stress to kick yourself off them, dealing an extra 2d6 damage and knocking back either yourself or the target to Very Close range."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/faun/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/firbolg",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Firbolg",
      "slug": "firbolg",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FIRBOLG",
        "lineStart": 2297,
        "lineEnd": 2307,
        "pdfPageStart": 35,
        "pdfPageEnd": 35
      },
      "description": "Firbolgs are bovine humanoids typically recognized by their broad noses and long, drooping ears. Some have faces that are a blend of humanoid and bison, ox, cow, or other bovine creatures. Others, often referred to as minotaurs, have heads that entirely resemble cattle. They are tall and muscular creatures, with heights ranging from around 5 feet to\n\n7 feet, and possess remarkable strength no matter their age. Some firbolgs are known to use this strength to charge their adversaries, an action that is particuarly effective for those who have one of the many varieties of horn styles commonly found in this ancestry. Though their unique characteristics can vary, all firbolgs are covered in fur, which can be muted and earth-toned in color, or come in a variety of pastels, such as soft pinks and blues. On average, firbolgs live for about 150 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Charge",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an Agility Roll to move from Far or Very Far range into Melee range with one or more targets, you can mark a Stress to deal 1d12 physical damage to all targets within Melee range."
        },
        {
          "name": "Unshakable",
          "rulesText": "When you would mark a Stress, roll a d6. On a result of 6, don’t mark it."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/firbolg/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/fungril",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Fungril",
      "slug": "fungril",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FUNGRIL",
        "lineStart": 2309,
        "lineEnd": 2317,
        "pdfPageStart": 35,
        "pdfPageEnd": 35
      },
      "description": "Fungril resemble humanoid mushrooms. They can be either more humanoid or more fungal in appearance, and they come in an assortment of colors, from earth tones to bright reds, yellows, purples, and blues. Fungril display an incredible variety of bodies, faces, and limbs, as there’s no single common shape among them. Even their heights range from a tiny 2 feet tall to a staggering 7 feet tall. While the common lifespan of a fungril is about 300 years, some have been reported to live much longer. They can communicate nonverbally, and many members of this ancestry use a mycelial array to chemically exchange information with other fungril across long distances.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Fungril Network",
          "rulesText": "Make an Instinct Roll (12) to use your mycelial array to speak with others of your ancestry. On a success, you can communicate across any distance."
        },
        {
          "name": "Death Connection",
          "rulesText": "While touching a corpse that died recently, you can mark a Stress to extract one memory from the corpse related to a specific emotion or sensation of your choice."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/fungril/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/galapa",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Galapa",
      "slug": "galapa",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GALAPA",
        "lineStart": 2319,
        "lineEnd": 2327,
        "pdfPageStart": 35,
        "pdfPageEnd": 35
      },
      "description": "Galapa resemble anthropomorphic turtles with large, domed shells into which they can retract. On average, they range from 4 feet to 6 feet in height, and their head and body shapes can resemble any type of turtle. Galapa come in a variety of earth tones—most often shades of green and brown— and possess unique patterns on their shells. Members of this ancestry can draw their head, arms, and legs into their shell for protection to use it as a natural shield when defensive measures are needed. Some supplement their shell's strength or appearance by attaching armor or carving unique designs, but the process is exceedingly painful. Most galapa move slowly no matter their age, and they can live approximately 150 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Shell",
          "rulesText": "Gain a bonus to your damage thresholds equal to your Proficiency."
        },
        {
          "name": "Retract",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to retract into your shell. While in your shell, you have resistance to physical damage, you have disadvantage on action rolls, and you can’t move."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/galapa/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/giant",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Giant",
      "slug": "giant",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GIANT",
        "lineStart": 2331,
        "lineEnd": 2339,
        "pdfPageStart": 36,
        "pdfPageEnd": 36
      },
      "description": "Giants are towering humanoids with broad shoulders, long arms, and one to three eyes. Adult giants range from 6 ½ to 8 ½ feet tall and are naturally muscular, regardless of body type. They are easily recognized by their wide frames and elongated arms and necks. Though they can have up to three eyes, all giants are born with none and remain sightless for their first year of life. Until a giant reaches the age of 10 and their features fully develop, the formation of their eyes may fluctuate. Those with a single eye are commonly known as cyclops. The average giant lifespan is about 75 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Endurance",
          "rulesText": "Gain an additional Hit Point slot at character creation."
        },
        {
          "name": "Reach",
          "rulesText": "Treat any weapon, ability, spell, or other feature that has a Melee range as though it has a Very Close range instead."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/giant/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/gnome",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Gnome",
      "slug": "gnome",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GNOME",
        "lineStart": 2341,
        "lineEnd": 2349,
        "pdfPageStart": 36,
        "pdfPageEnd": 36
      },
      "description": "Gnomes are typically small humanoids with conical heads and the ability to teleport short distances. While the average gnome is about 3 feet in height, their proportionately long arms and large hands give them an extended reach for their size. In contrast, their square frames have shorter torsos and legs. Alongside these characteristics, gnomes are easily identified by their softly pointed ears and wide eyes. Their heads are large, and the conical shape varies by family. Gnomes also inherit their minor teleportation ability from their parents, with families exhibiting their own manifestations when disappearing and reappearing. These effects can include small clouds of smoke, motes of light, or simply appearing to evaporate into thin air. Gnomes are known to begin grasping and holding objects from a very young age, and their hands remain dextrous over their 200-year lifespan even when their bodies have slowed from age.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Nimble Fingers",
          "rulesText": "When you make a Finesse Roll, you can spend 2 Hope to reroll your Hope Die."
        },
        {
          "name": "Flicker Step",
          "rulesText": "Once per scene, you can teleport to another point you can see within Far range."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/gnome/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/goblin",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Goblin",
      "slug": "goblin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GOBLIN",
        "lineStart": 2351,
        "lineEnd": 2359,
        "pdfPageStart": 36,
        "pdfPageEnd": 36
      },
      "description": "Goblins are small humanoids easily recognizable by their large eyes and massive membranous ears. With keen hearing and sharp eyesight, they perceive details both at great distances and in darkness, allowing them to move through less-optimal environments with ease. Their skin and eye colors are incredibly varied, with no one hue, either vibrant or subdued, more dominant than another. A typical goblin stands between 3 feet and 4 feet tall, and each of their ears is about the size of their head. Goblins are known to use ear positions to very specific effect when communicating nonverbally. A goblin’s lifespan is roughly 100 years, and many maintain their keen hearing and sight well into advanced age.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Surefooted",
          "rulesText": "You ignore disadvantage on Agility Rolls."
        },
        {
          "name": "Danger Sense",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest, mark a Stress to force an adversary to reroll an attack against you or an ally within Very Close range."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/goblin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/halfling",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Halfling",
      "slug": "halfling",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "HALFLING",
        "lineStart": 2361,
        "lineEnd": 2369,
        "pdfPageStart": 36,
        "pdfPageEnd": 36
      },
      "description": "Halflings are small humanoids with large hairy feet and prominent rounded ears. On average, halflings are 3 to 4 feet in height, and their ears, nose, and feet are larger in proportion to the rest of their body. Members of this ancestry live for around 150 years, and a halfling’s appearance is likely to remain youthful even as they progress from adulthood into old age. Halflings are naturally attuned to the magnetic fields of the Mortal Realm, granting them a strong internal compass. They also possess acute senses of hearing and smell, and can often detect those who are familiar to them by the sound of their movements.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Luckbringer",
          "rulesText": "At the start of each session, everyone in your party gains a Hope."
        },
        {
          "name": "Internal Compass",
          "rulesText": "When you roll a 1 on your Hope Die, you can reroll it."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/halfling/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/human",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Human",
      "slug": "human",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "HUMAN",
        "lineStart": 2371,
        "lineEnd": 2379,
        "pdfPageStart": 36,
        "pdfPageEnd": 36
      },
      "description": "Humans are most easily recognized by their dexterous hands, rounded ears, and bodies built for endurance. Their average height ranges from just under 5 feet to about 6 ½ feet. They have a wide variety of builds, with some being quite broad, others lithe, and many inhabiting the spectrum in between. Humans are physically adaptable and adjust to harsh climates with relative ease. In general, humans live to an age of about 100, with their bodies changing dramatically between their youngest and oldest years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "High Stamina",
          "rulesText": "Gain an additional Stress slot at character creation."
        },
        {
          "name": "Adaptability",
          "rulesText": "When you fail a roll that utilized one of your Experiences, you can mark a Stress to reroll."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/human/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/infernis",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Infernis",
      "slug": "infernis",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "INFERNIS",
        "lineStart": 2383,
        "lineEnd": 2393,
        "pdfPageStart": 37,
        "pdfPageEnd": 37
      },
      "description": "Infernis are humanoids who possess sharp canine teeth, pointed ears, and horns. They are the descendants of demons from the Circles Below. On average, infernis range in height from 5 feet to 7 feet and are known to have long fingers and pointed nails. Some have long, thin, and smooth tails that end in points, forks, or arrowheads. It’s common for infernis to have two or four horns—though some have crowns of many horns, or only one. These horns can also grow asymmetrically, forming unique, often curving, shapes that infernis enhance with carving and ornamentation. Their skin, hair, and horns come in an assortment of colors that can include soft pastels, stark tones, or vibrant hues, such as rosy scarlet, deep purple, and pitch black.\n\nInfernis possess a “dread visage” that manifests both involuntarily, such as when they experience fear or other strong emotions, or purposefully, such as when they wish to intimidate an adversary. This visage can briefly modify their appearance in a variety of ways, including lengthening their teeth and nails, changing the colors of their eyes, twisting their horns, or enhancing their height. On average, infernis live up to 350 years, with some attributing this lifespan to their demonic lineage.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Fearless",
          "rulesText": "When you roll with Fear, you can mark 2 Stress to change it into a roll with Hope instead."
        },
        {
          "name": "Dread Visage",
          "rulesText": "You have advantage on rolls to intimidate hostile creatures."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/infernis/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/katari",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Katari",
      "slug": "katari",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "KATARI",
        "lineStart": 2395,
        "lineEnd": 2403,
        "pdfPageStart": 37,
        "pdfPageEnd": 37
      },
      "description": "Katari are feline humanoids with retractable claws, vertically slit pupils, and high, triangular ears. They can also have small, pointed canine teeth, soft fur, and long whiskers that assist their perception and navigation. Their ears can swivel nearly 180 degrees to detect sound, adding to their heightened senses. Katari may look more or less feline or humanoid, with catlike attributes in the form of hair, whiskers, and a muzzle. About half of the katari population have tails. Their skin and fur come in a wide range of hues and patterns, including solid colors, calico tones, tabby stripes, and an array of spots, patches, marbling, or bands. Their height ranges from about 3 feet to 6 ½ feet, and they live to around 150 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Feline Instincts",
          "rulesText": "When you make an Agility Roll, you can spend 2 Hope to reroll your Hope Die."
        },
        {
          "name": "Retracting Claws",
          "rulesText": "Make an Agility Roll to scratch a target within Melee range. On a success, they become temporarily Vulnerable."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/katari/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/mixed-ancestry",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Mixed Ancestry",
      "slug": "mixed-ancestry",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "MIXED ANCESTRY",
        "lineStart": 2437,
        "lineEnd": 2448,
        "pdfPageStart": 38,
        "pdfPageEnd": 38
      },
      "description": "Families within the world of Daggerheart are as unique as the peoples and cultures that inhabit it. Anyone’s appearance and abilities can be shaped by blood, magic, proximity, or a variety of other factors.\n\nIf you decide that your character is a descendant of multiple ancestries and you want to mechanically represent that in the game, use the steps below:\n\n1. Determine Ancestry Combination When you choose an ancestry at character creation, write down how your character identifies themself in the Heritage section of your character sheet. For example, if your character is descended from both goblins and orcs, you could use a hybridized term, such as “goblin-orc,” to describe your ancestry, list only the ancestry you more closely identify with (e.g., just “goblin” or just “orc”), or invent a new term, such as “toothling.”\n2. Choose Ancestry Features Work with your GM to choose two features from the ancestries in your character’s lineage. You must choose the first feature from one ancestry and the second from another. Write both down on a notecard you can keep with your other cards or next to your character sheet.\n\nFor example, if you are making a goblin-orc, you might take the “Surefooted” and “Tusks” features or the “Sturdy” and “Danger Sense” features. You can’t take both the “Surefooted” and “Sturdy” features, because these are both the first features listed on their respective ancestry cards.\n\nYour character's heritage might include more than two ancestries, but you still only choose features from two. You can represent additional ancestries through their appearance or backstory.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/mixed-ancestry/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/orc",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Orc",
      "slug": "orc",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ORC",
        "lineStart": 2405,
        "lineEnd": 2413,
        "pdfPageStart": 37,
        "pdfPageEnd": 37
      },
      "description": "Orcs are humanoids most easily recognized by their square features and boar-like tusks that protrude from their lower jaw. Tusks come in various sizes, and though they extend from the mouth, they aren’t used for consuming food. Instead, many orcs choose to decorate their tusks with significant ornamentation. Orcs typically live for 125 years, and unless altered, their tusks continue to grow throughout the course of their lives. Their ears are pointed, and their hair and skin typically have green, blue, pink, or gray tones. Orcs tend toward a muscular build, and their average height ranges from 5 feet to 6 ½ feet.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Sturdy",
          "rulesText": "When you have 1 Hit Point remaining, attacks against you have disadvantage."
        },
        {
          "name": "Tusks",
          "rulesText": "When you succeed on an attack against a target within Melee range, you can spend a Hope to gore the target with your tusks, dealing an extra 1d6 damage."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/orc/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/ribbet",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Ribbet",
      "slug": "ribbet",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "RIBBET",
        "lineStart": 2415,
        "lineEnd": 2423,
        "pdfPageStart": 37,
        "pdfPageEnd": 37
      },
      "description": "Ribbets resemble anthropomorphic frogs with protruding eyes and webbed hands and feet. They have smooth (though sometimes warty) moist skin and eyes positioned on either side of their head. Some ribbets have hind legs more than twice the length of their torso, while others have short limbs. No matter their size (which ranges from about 3 feet to 4 ½ feet), ribbets primarily move by hopping. All ribbets have webbed appendages, allowing them to swim with ease. Some ribbets possess a natural green-and-brown camouflage, while others are vibrantly colored with bold patterns. No matter their appearance, all ribbets are born from eggs laid in the water, hatch into tadpoles, and after about 6 to 7 years, grow into amphibians that can move around on land. Ribbets live for approximately 100 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Amphibious",
          "rulesText": "You can breathe and move naturally underwater."
        },
        {
          "name": "Long Tongue",
          "rulesText": "You can use your long tongue to grab onto things within Close range. Mark a Stress to use your tongue as a Finesse Close weapon that deals d12 physical damage using your Proficiency."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/ribbet/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/simiah",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Simiah",
      "slug": "simiah",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SIMIAH",
        "lineStart": 2427,
        "lineEnd": 2435,
        "pdfPageStart": 38,
        "pdfPageEnd": 38
      },
      "description": "Simiah resemble anthropomorphic monkeys and apes with long limbs and prehensile feet. While their appearance reflects all simian creatures, from the largest gorilla to the smallest marmoset, their size does not align with their animal counterparts, and they can be anywhere from 2 to 6 feet tall. All simiah can use their dexterous feet for nonverbal communication, work, and combat. Additionally, some also have prehensile tails that can grasp objects or help with balance during difficult maneuvers. These traits grant members of this ancestry unique agility that aids them in a variety of physical tasks. In particular, simiah are skilled climbers and can easily transition from bipedal movement to knuckle-walking and climbing, and back again. On average, simiah live for about 100 years.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Natural Climber",
          "rulesText": "You have advantage on Agility Rolls that involve balancing and climbing."
        },
        {
          "name": "Nimble",
          "rulesText": "Gain a permanent +1 bonus to your Evasion at character creation."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/simiah/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/skykin",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Skykin",
      "slug": "skykin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SKYKIN",
        "lineStart": 2237,
        "lineEnd": 2247,
        "pdfPageStart": 34,
        "pdfPageEnd": 34
      },
      "description": "Skykin are humanoids whose bodies are made of flesh and air. Descended from air elementals, skykin have color-shifting skin that reflects the pale grays and blues of a clear morning sky, the smoldering oranges and fuchsias of the setting sun, or the deep sables and purples of a star-dappled night. These kin often have semitranslucent skin, floating hair of wispy smoke or clouds, or small breezes constantly fluttering their clothing. The intensity of these characteristics heightens or lessens based on the strength of their emotions. Skykin grow to be 5½ to 7 feet on average, and some have elongated features, including their pointed ears, necks, hands, feet, or limbs.\n\nSkykin are born as wisps of air or small clouds, often hard to see and hold. Until they mature and become more solid, they’re contained in jars and other such vessels. Though skykin become more humanoid throughout adolescence, their bodies remain flexible, and some adults can adjust their shape throughout their lives, though it takes time and effort. Skykin live for approximately 300 years, and when they breathe their last, they disappear into the air.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Gale Force",
          "rulesText": "Mark a Stress to conjure a gust of wind that carries you or a Very Close ally up to Very Far range. Additionally, you can always control the speed at which you fall."
        },
        {
          "name": "Eye of the Storm",
          "rulesText": "Spend 2 Hope to grant you or an ally within Melee range a +1 bonus to Evasion until you take Severe damage or you use this feature again."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/skykin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/ancestries/tidekin",
      "@type": "Ancestry",
      "name": "Tidekin",
      "slug": "tidekin",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "TIDEKIN",
        "lineStart": 2249,
        "lineEnd": 2259,
        "pdfPageStart": 34,
        "pdfPageEnd": 34
      },
      "description": "Tidekin are humanoids whose bodies are made of flesh and water. Descended from water elementals, tidekin possess finlike ears and webbing between their digits. The color of their skin, hair, and eyes reflects that of water’s many forms, with some exhibiting bright shades of aquas, greens, and seafoams like shallow rivers or tropical oceans, while others display dark blues and purples from the deepest oceans. For some, these colors shift beneath semitranslucent skin like waves. Tidekin might have water formations that adorn their heads like hair or waves that shift and move across their body. Instead of expressing emotions through tears, their water formations change with their emotional state, and they can purposefully or accidentally use their shifting colors or water formations as a method of nonverbal communication.\n\nTidekin are born as a small amount of water, such as a puddle or liquid orb. As children, they’re often kept in watertight containers such as bowls and bottles, and they tend to have a fluid appearance throughout their childhood until they gain a more solid humanoid form around adolescence. Adult tidekin range from 5 to 6½ feet in height, though their general body shape is influenced by the fluidity of water and might change, with some effort, over their approximately 300-year lifespan. When members of this ancestry die, they dissolve into water and flow away.",
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Amphibious",
          "rulesText": "You can breathe and move naturally underwater."
        },
        {
          "name": "Lifespring",
          "rulesText": "Once per rest when you have access to a small amount of water, you can mark a Stress to clear a Hit Point on yourself or an ally within Very Close range."
        }
      ],
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/ancestries/tidekin/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/duneborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Duneborne",
      "slug": "duneborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DUNEBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2456,
        "lineEnd": 2464,
        "pdfPageStart": 38,
        "pdfPageEnd": 38
      },
      "description": "Being part of a duneborne community means you’ve made a home among the shifting sands and arid climate of the desert. Each duneborne enclave develops specific methods of protecting themselves from the blazing sun, moving across the scorching sands, and acquiring and storing precious resources. Some duneborne travel their desert homes, maneuvering through the dunes herding livestock, while others cluster around oases, collaborating with one another to maintain and share a rare area of abundant resources. Duneborne settlements are known for architecture that captures wind to cool their homes and irrigation systems that tap into underground reservoirs and tend to crops even amidst the harshest conditions. Many duneborne put added emphasis on familial bonds and the need for everyone to contribute to the well-being of the collective.\n\nDuneborne are often adaptable, centered, collaborative, devoted, cheerful, and observant.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Oasis",
        "rulesText": "During a short rest, you or an ally can reroll a die used for a downtime move and take the higher result."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/duneborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/freeborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Freeborne",
      "slug": "freeborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FREEBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2466,
        "lineEnd": 2478,
        "pdfPageStart": 38,
        "pdfPageEnd": 39
      },
      "description": "Being part of a freeborne community means you’re from a collective that once lived under tyrannical rule but is now liberated. Though no two freeborne enclaves are the same, all are unified by the experience of fighting to preserve their culture while others tried to eradicate it. Whether due to their religion, resources, location, or culture, freeborne groups were once subjugated by another community. For some, this subjugation might have only recently ended; for others, even if their freedom has been long-won, the scars of tyranny can last, bonding together families and neighbors for decades or even generations. Many freeborne collectives unify in and rebuild the lands reclaimed from their oppressors or in a homeland they were taken from. Others see “home” as a collection of people who care for one another, no matter where they reside.\n\nMany freeborne passionately celebrate their unique holidays, art, food, decor, and other customs, whether they’re enjoying long-held traditions or new practices born from liberation. Their past experiences often forge in them a fierce protectiveness of their community and an equally strong fervor to celebrate their culture.\n\nFreeborne are often optimistic, guarded, courageous, joyful, subversive, and traumatized.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Unbound",
        "rulesText": "Once per session when you roll with Fear, you can change it into a roll with Hope instead."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/freeborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/frostborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Frostborne",
      "slug": "frostborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "FROSTBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2480,
        "lineEnd": 2488,
        "pdfPageStart": 39,
        "pdfPageEnd": 39
      },
      "description": "Being part of a frostborne community means you come from a place of snow and ice. Forged in bitter and unrelenting cold, frostborne communities are accustomed to surviving in the harshest conditions. The glaciers and tundras they call home usually lack wood, so these collectives use more readily available materials such as ice, stone, bone, and animal pelts to make shelter and conserve heat. They are adept at ensuring their supplies go a long way and that all their people have access to the essentials needed to survive. As part of their systems of survival, many frostborne closely track weather patterns, the rising and setting of the sun, and the needs of local flora and fauna. Because of the howling wind and biting snow, some frostborne communities develop nonverbal means of communication so they can cover their faces for safety during dangerous weather.\n\nFrostborne are often cunning, gregarious, intrepid, purposeful, quiet, and warm.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Hardy",
        "rulesText": "When you take a rest, you clear a Hit Point."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/frostborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/hearthborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Hearthborne",
      "slug": "hearthborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "HEARTHBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2490,
        "lineEnd": 2498,
        "pdfPageStart": 39,
        "pdfPageEnd": 39
      },
      "description": "Being part of a hearthborne community means you come from humble origins, having lived in a modest village or quaint countryside. These communities are often small groups that live in resource-rich, peaceful areas that allow families and neighbors the time to develop close-knit relationships rather than compete over scarce supplies. Hearthborne communities are largely agrarian and pastoral, focusing the layout of their villages around their plants and animals. The town centers typically feature a collection of locally owned shops with skilled artisans such as blacksmiths, cobblers, alchemists, and the like. Sometimes, hearthborne communities have distant but powerful rulers who own the land the villagers work and live on—in those cases, resources are controlled by the wealthy even though the labor is performed by locals, and workers might struggle to afford the basics they need to survive. While some hearthborne individuals can be mistrustful of outsiders, most are known for being welcoming, peaceful, and hospitable.\n\nHearthborne are often careful, generous, insular, loquacious, smiley, and traditional.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Close-Knit",
        "rulesText": "Once per long rest, you can spend any number of Hope to grant an ally within Far range an equal number of Hope."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/hearthborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/highborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Highborne",
      "slug": "highborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "HIGHBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2500,
        "lineEnd": 2508,
        "pdfPageStart": 39,
        "pdfPageEnd": 39
      },
      "description": "Being part of a highborne community means you're accustomed to a life of elegance, opulence, and prestige within the upper echelons of society. Traditionally, members of a highborne community possess incredible material wealth. While this can take a variety of forms depending on the community—including gold and other minerals, land, or controlling the means of production—this status always comes with power and influence. Highborne place great value on titles and possessions, and there is little social mobility within their ranks. Members of a highborne community often control the political and economic status of the areas in which they live due to their ability to influence people and the economy with their substantial wealth. The health and safety of the less affluent people who live in these locations often hinges on the ability of this highborne ruling class to prioritize the well-being of their subjects over profit.\n\nHighborne are often amiable, candid, conniving, enterprising, ostentatious, and unflappable.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Privilege",
        "rulesText": "You have advantage on rolls to consort with nobles, negotiate prices, or leverage your reputation to get what you want."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/highborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/loreborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Loreborne",
      "slug": "loreborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "LOREBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2510,
        "lineEnd": 2518,
        "pdfPageStart": 39,
        "pdfPageEnd": 39
      },
      "description": "Being part of a loreborne community means you’re from a society that favors strong academic or political prowess. Loreborne communities highly value knowledge, frequently in the form of historical preservation, political advancement, scientific study, skill development, or lore and mythology compilation. Most members of these communities research in institutions built in bastions of civilization, while some eclectic few thrive in gathering information from the natural world. Some may be isolationists, operating in smaller enclaves, schools, or guilds and following their own unique ethos. Others still wield their knowledge on a larger scale, making deft political maneuvers across governmental landscapes.\n\nLoreborne are often direct, eloquent, inquisitive, patient, rhapsodic, and witty.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Well-Read",
        "rulesText": "You have advantage on rolls that involve the history, culture, or politics of a prominent person or place."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/loreborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/orderborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Orderborne",
      "slug": "orderborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "ORDERBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2522,
        "lineEnd": 2530,
        "pdfPageStart": 40,
        "pdfPageEnd": 40
      },
      "description": "Being part of an orderborne community means you’re from a collective that focuses on discipline or faith, and you uphold a set of principles that reflect your experience there. Orderborne are frequently some of the most powerful among the surrounding communities. By aligning the members of their society around a common value or goal, such as a god, doctrine, ethos, or even a shared business or trade, the ruling bodies of these enclaves can mobilize larger populations with less effort. While orderborne communities take a variety of forms—some even profoundly pacifistic—perhaps the most feared are those that structure themselves around military prowess. In such a case, it’s not uncommon for orderborne to provide soldiers for hire to other cities or countries.\n\nOrderborne are often ambitious, benevolent, pensive, prudent, sardonic, and stoic.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Dedicated",
        "rulesText": "Record three sayings or values your upbringing instilled in you. Once per rest, when you describe how you’re embodying one of these principles through your current action, you can roll a d20 as your Hope Die."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/orderborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/reborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Reborne",
      "slug": "reborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "REBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2532,
        "lineEnd": 2546,
        "pdfPageStart": 40,
        "pdfPageEnd": 40
      },
      "description": "You were once a member of a different community that you’re no longer part of. Unlike members of collectives who are allied by familiarity, shared location, history, and culture, reborne are individuals who might never meet and are unified only by their shared experience of solitude. Whether they left by choice or were ripped from their community, all reborne are disconnected from the place where they once belonged, and they carry that wound with them.\n\nSome reborne are isolated from their community because they chose to reject or renounce it, or they could have been forcibly expelled by other members. These instances are often due to a conflict, such as clashing ideological differences, a falling out with their people, or an incident that forced them to depart. Other reborne have no knowledge of their culture, either due to their upbringing or perhaps because they lost their memory through injury or magic. Some have even returned from beyond the veil after death with no recollection of their previous life—or the culture that shaped it.\n\nOften, reborne individuals tirelessly search for their past or feel that they’ll never belong in a collective, but all are afforded the opportunity to turn toward the future and redefine themselves while building new bonds with those they come to know and love.\n\nReborne are often curious, hopeful, innocent, isolated, mournful, and uncanny.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Found Family",
        "rulesText": "Once per rest, you can spend a Hope to use an ally’s Experience as if it were your own. When you do so, describe how your time with that ally prepared you for this moment.\n\nWhen you join a new community or rediscover your old one, you can permanently trade this card for that one instead."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/reborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/ridgeborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Ridgeborne",
      "slug": "ridgeborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "RIDGEBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2548,
        "lineEnd": 2556,
        "pdfPageStart": 40,
        "pdfPageEnd": 40
      },
      "description": "Being part of a ridgeborne community means you’ve called the rocky peaks and sharp cliffs of the mountainside home. Those who’ve lived in the mountains often consider themselves hardier than most because they’ve thrived among the most dangerous terrain many continents have to offer. These groups are adept at adaptation, developing unique technologies and equipment to move both people and products across difficult terrain. As such, ridgeborne grow up scrambling and climbing, making them sturdy and strong-willed. Ridgeborne localities appear in a variety of forms—some cities carve out entire cliff faces, others construct castles of stone, and still more live in small homes on windblown peaks. Outside forces often struggle to attack ridgeborne groups, as the small militias and large military forces of the mountains are adept at utilizing their high-ground advantage.\n\nRidgeborne are often bold, hardy, indomitable, loyal, reserved, and stubborn.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Steady",
        "rulesText": "You have advantage on rolls to traverse dangerous cliffs and ledges, navigate harsh environments, and use your survival knowledge."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/ridgeborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/seaborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Seaborne",
      "slug": "seaborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SEABORNE",
        "lineStart": 2558,
        "lineEnd": 2568,
        "pdfPageStart": 40,
        "pdfPageEnd": 41
      },
      "description": "Being part of a seaborne community means you lived on or near a large body of water. Seaborne communities are built, both physically and culturally, around the specific waters they call home. Some of these groups live along the shore, constructing ports for locals and travelers alike. These harbors function as centers of commerce, tourist attractions, or even just a safe place to lay down one’s head after weeks of travel. Other seaborne live on the water in small boats or large ships, with the idea of “home” comprising a ship and its crew, rather than any one landmass. No matter their exact location, seaborne communities are closely tied to the ocean tides and the creatures who inhabit them. Seaborne learn to fish at a young age, and train from birth to hold their breath and swim in even the most tumultuous waters. Individuals from these groups are highly sought after for their sailing skills, and many become captains of vessels, whether within their own community, working for another, or even at the helm of a powerful naval operation.\n\nSeaborne are often candid, cooperative, exuberant, fierce, resolute, and weathered.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Know the Tide",
        "rulesText": "You can sense the ebb and flow of life. When you roll with Fear, place a token on your community card. You can hold a number of tokens equal to your level. Before you make an action roll, you can spend any number of these tokens to gain a +1 bonus to the roll for each token spent. At the end of each session, clear all unspent tokens."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/seaborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/slyborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Slyborne",
      "slug": "slyborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SLYBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2570,
        "lineEnd": 2578,
        "pdfPageStart": 41,
        "pdfPageEnd": 41
      },
      "description": "Being part of a slyborne community means you come from a group that operates outside the law, including all manner of criminals, grifters, and con artists. Members of slyborne communities are brought together by their disreputable goals and their clever means of achieving them. Many people in these communities have an array of unscrupulous skills: forging, thievery, smuggling, and violence. People of any social class can be slyborne, from those who have garnered vast wealth and influence to those without a coin to their name. To the outside eye, slyborne might appear to be ruffians with no loyalty, but these communities possess some of the strictest codes of honor which, when broken, can result in a terrifying end for the transgressor.\n\nSlyborne are often calculating, clever, formidable, perceptive, shrewd, and tenacious.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Scoundrel",
        "rulesText": "You have advantage on rolls to negotiate with criminals, detect lies, or find a safe place to hide."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/slyborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/underborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Underborne",
      "slug": "underborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "UNDERBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2580,
        "lineEnd": 2588,
        "pdfPageStart": 41,
        "pdfPageEnd": 41
      },
      "description": "Being part of an underborne community means you’re from a subterranean society. Many underborne live right beneath the cities and villages of other collectives, while some live much deeper. These communities range from small family groups in burrows to massive metropolises in caverns of stone. In many locales, underborne are recognized for their incredible boldness and skill that enable great feats of architecture and engineering. Underborne are regularly hired for their bravery, as even the least daring among them has likely encountered formidable belowground beasts, and learning to dispatch such creatures is common practice amongst these societies. Because of the dangers of their environment, many underborne communities develop unique nonverbal languages that prove equally useful on the surface.\n\nUnderborne are often composed, elusive, indomitable, innovative, resourceful, and unpretentious.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Low-Light Living",
        "rulesText": "When you’re in an area with low light or heavy shadow, you have advantage on rolls to hide, investigate, or perceive details within that area."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/underborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/wanderborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Wanderborne",
      "slug": "wanderborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WANDERBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2590,
        "lineEnd": 2598,
        "pdfPageStart": 41,
        "pdfPageEnd": 41
      },
      "description": "Being part of a wanderborne community means you’ve lived as a nomad, forgoing a permanent home and experiencing a wide variety of cultures. Unlike many communities that are defined by their locale, wanderborne are defined by their traveling lifestyle. Because of their frequent migration, wanderborne put less value on the accumulation of material possessions in favor of acquiring information, skills, and connections. While some wanderborne are allied by a common ethos, such as a religion or a set of political or economic values, others come together after shared tragedy, such as the loss of their home or land. No matter the reason, the dangers posed by life on the road and the choice to continue down that road together mean that wanderborne are known for their unwavering loyalty.\n\nWanderborne are often inscrutable, magnanimous, mirthful, reliable, savvy, and unorthodox.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Nomadic Pack",
        "rulesText": "Add a Nomadic Pack to your inventory. Once per session, you can spend a Hope to reach into this pack and pull out a mundane item that’s useful to your situation. Work with the GM to figure out what item you take out."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/wanderborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/warborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Warborne",
      "slug": "warborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WARBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2600,
        "lineEnd": 2610,
        "pdfPageStart": 41,
        "pdfPageEnd": 41
      },
      "description": "Being part of a warborne community means you come from a place that is, or was, ravaged by war. These groups might be composed of civilians who live in an active warzone, soldiers who served together in the armed forces, or citizens of nations that have been in conflict for decades. Whether civilian or military, warborne are typically skilled in self-defense, even if their tactics are focused on avoidance rather than offensive action.\n\nWarborne defending their homeland are known for using their environment to their advantage, while invading warborne might rely entirely on their military might. No matter their origin, the life of a warborne is defined at least in part by resourcefulness. Those who live in an occupied territory might need to undertake massive reconstruction efforts to repair and preserve homes, farms, workplaces, or cultural monuments— the remnants of one building often become the materials to repair another. Meanwhile, warborne who are part of an invading army typically carry few personal possessions and must rely on scavenging the warzones they occupy. Whether they find themselves on the winning or losing side of a conflict, those from warborne communities are likely to bear trauma for the rest of their lives, their sense of safety and mortality forever marred by the mass casualties and destruction of war.\n\nWarborne are often strategic, devastated, determined, calculating, unwavering, and devoted.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Brave Face",
        "rulesText": "Once per session when you would be forced to mark a Stress, you can spend a Hope instead.."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/warborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/communities/wildborne",
      "@type": "Community",
      "name": "Wildborne",
      "slug": "wildborne",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WILDBORNE",
        "lineStart": 2614,
        "lineEnd": 2622,
        "pdfPageStart": 42,
        "pdfPageEnd": 42
      },
      "description": "Being part of a wildborne community means you lived deep within the forest. Wildborne communities are defined by their dedication to the conservation of their homelands, and many have strong religious or cultural ties to the fauna they live among. This results in unique architectural and technological advancements that favor sustainability over short-term, high-yield results. It is a hallmark of wildborne societies to integrate their villages and cities with the natural environment and avoid disturbing the lives of the plants and animals. While some construct their lodgings high in the branches of trees, others establish their homes on the ground beneath the forest canopy. It’s not uncommon for wildborne to remain reclusive and hidden within their woodland homes.\n\nWildborne are often hardy, loyal, nurturing, reclusive, sagacious, and vibrant.",
      "feature": {
        "name": "Lightfoot",
        "rulesText": "Your movement is naturally silent. You have advantage on rolls to move without being heard."
      },
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/communities/wildborne/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/demigod",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Demigod",
      "slug": "demigod",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "DEMIGOD",
        "lineStart": 2646,
        "lineEnd": 2665,
        "pdfPageStart": 43,
        "pdfPageEnd": 43
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Gifted",
          "rulesText": "You gain a +1 bonus to action, reaction, and damage rolls."
        },
        {
          "name": "Weight of Divinity",
          "rulesText": "When you fail a roll, you must mark a Stress or the GM gains a Fear."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "Your divinity has affected an aspect of your appearance. In what way do you look different from others?",
        "Who bestowed demigod status upon you, and what trial did you complete to earn this gift?",
        "You were made fun of as a child for your divine parentage. Why?",
        "Why was the knowledge that you are a demigod kept from you for many years? Who kept this secret?",
        "You have an obligation you must fulfill due to the immortal blood that runs in your veins. What is this duty?",
        "In what way did your divinity initially manifest? Why did it surprise you?"
      ],
      "description": "Demigods are mortal creatures whose veins flow with the blood of the gods. They typically gain their power from divine parentage, accomplishing an incredible feat, or receiving a gift from a god. Many demigods appear as a typical member of their mortal ancestry, but all possess a subtle luminosity and a gleam in their eyes. They’re also known to have pearlescent or metallic skin, and they might grow in size or gain physical features related to their power when it reveals itself. Because they share the blood of a Faint Divinity, a Forgotten God, a New God, or any other deity, no two demigods look the same.\n\nDemigods are bestowed with a fragment of deific power, but not everything a demigod inherits is a blessing. Though they often excel at tasks they’re well suited for, the expectations set upon them are often difficult and costly to live up to.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/demigod/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/ghost",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Ghost",
      "slug": "ghost",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "GHOST",
        "lineStart": 2667,
        "lineEnd": 2688,
        "pdfPageStart": 43,
        "pdfPageEnd": 43
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Unfinished Business",
          "rulesText": "Work with your GM to decide what purpose or desire keeps you bound to the Mortal Realm. When you fulfill it, you cross through the veil of death."
        },
        {
          "name": "Ephemeral",
          "rulesText": "Your body wavers in and out of being corporeal. You are resistant to physical damage, take double magic damage, and can mark 2 Stress to momentarily pass through a solid object."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "How did you die, and what fear has your death instilled in you?",
        "What brought you back from beyond the veil of death?",
        "In life, you loved someone dearly. Why do you need to find them again?",
        "What must you accomplish now that you’ve returned to the Mortal Realm? How will you meet your goal?",
        "Who harmed you in your first life? How do you plan to deal with them now that you’ve returned?",
        "You had to sacrifice something dear to you in order to come back to the Mortal Realm. What did you give up, and who did you give it to?"
      ],
      "description": "Ghosts are the spirits of the once-living who are bound to the Mortal Realm. They could be called forth after passing through the veil of death with necromantic rites, alchemical experimentation, the desperation of a loved one—or they may never have passed through the veil at all. Those who met a horrific end, were killed with a cursed weapon, or died in a place of power might have become trapped in the Mortal Realm instead of passing on.\n\nBecause they occupy the space between life and death, a ghost can shift between the physical and the spectral and, at times, pass through solid objects. While some ghosts are almost indistinguishable from the living, others emanate spectral auras, appear desaturated in color, or bear physical evidence of their cause of death. When a ghost becomes incorporeal— which can occur at will, due to heightened emotions, or seemingly at random—they might appear semitranslucent, like a breath of fog that could dissipate in the wind.\n\nSome ghosts return for revenge, some to save a loved one, and others to meet a goal or discover hidden knowledge. Their drive to accomplish this goal is the force that allows them to exist in the Mortal Realm. When they complete that goal, their work is done, and they immediately pass beyond the veil of death.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/ghost/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/reanimated",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Reanimated",
      "slug": "reanimated",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "REANIMATED",
        "lineStart": 2692,
        "lineEnd": 2711,
        "pdfPageStart": 44,
        "pdfPageEnd": 44
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Corpse",
          "rulesText": "During a rest, you can clear Hit Points only if you have access to remains from a recently deceased creature. Describe how you use these materials to maintain your corpse."
        },
        {
          "name": "Won’t Stay Dead",
          "rulesText": "When you choose the Risk It All death move and fail, you can permanently mark a Hit Point to succeed instead. When you do, you still use the Hope Die’s value to clear Hit Points and Stress. When you permanently mark your last Hit Point, you pass through the veil of death."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "How did you first die, and how has that death affected your view on life?",
        "A fragment of memory from your first life haunts you. What is the memory, and why do you think it’s important?",
        "You had a dream for your life that you now believe you can never reach. What was your dream, and why do you think being reanimated makes it impossible to fulfill?",
        "How were you brought back from the dead, and how did the process change your body?",
        "Someone brought you back from death. Who were they, and how do you feel about this person and what they did to you?",
        "In the brief time you passed through the veil of death, you saw something that changed you forever. What was it?"
      ],
      "description": "Reanimated are corpses who have been brought back to life. Whether through a spell, a holy ritual, arcane experimentation, or other unnatural methods, these mortals have returned from beyond the veil of death as undead and are bound to their decaying bodies. Newly reanimated are more likely to look like a typical member of their ancestry, but as they go on, they must patch and repair their bodies with bones, flesh, and other materials harvested from the recently deceased. While some repair their wounds and decay with needle, thread, and herbs, others use magic to incorporate these materials into themselves.\n\nMany stories of the reanimated describe them as possessing increased durability or, in some cases, immunity to pain. This may be true for some reanimated, but many experience prolonged agony—whether from the physical echoes of their own demise, the discomfort of moving a decomposing body, the trouble controlling their amalgamated parts, or the mental anguish of being forcibly pulled back from death into a second life.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/reanimated/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/shapeshifter",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Shapeshifter",
      "slug": "shapeshifter",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "SHAPESHIFTER",
        "lineStart": 2713,
        "lineEnd": 2734,
        "pdfPageStart": 44,
        "pdfPageEnd": 44
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Change Shape",
          "rulesText": "During a rest, you can use a downtime move to swap your current ancestry with another. When you do, describe how your appearance changes."
        },
        {
          "name": "Only Skin Deep",
          "rulesText": "You gain the benefit of only one of your chosen ancestry’s features, which you select when you choose the ancestry. You can use a downtime move to choose a different feature from that ancestry."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "You believe you have a “true form,” but you haven’t seen it. What do you think it is, and how do you know it’s there?",
        "What trick have you developed to identify other shapeshifters?",
        "Someone you loved dearly abandoned you when they found out about your ability to change shape. Who was it, and what happened?",
        "What is one form you’ve vowed to never take, and why?",
        "Which ancestry do you live as more than others? Why do you spend so much time in this form?",
        "Someone you know can instantly recognize you, no matter your form. How do they claim to identify you and what does their familiarity mean to you?"
      ],
      "description": "Shapeshifters are creatures who can change their physical form. Whether they were born with this ability, gained it as a result of magical craft or a curse, or received this power from an otherworldly entity, shapeshifters have a unique relationship with their identity. Because shapeshifters can transform into any ancestry—one moment living as a small and nimble faerie and the next as a large and tough galapa— their experiences with the outside world can vary greatly from day to day.\n\nWhile a shapeshifter is typically indistinguishable from another member of the ancestry they transform into, each shapeshifter has one characteristic that doesn’t change no matter their form, such as their eyes, their coloring, or a unique birthmark. They also can’t mimic a specific person’s appearance, as they become a different version of themselves in a new body.\n\nTaking on a new form also doesn’t allow them to immediately have or use all the new form’s characteristics—they must focus their efforts on the physical characteristics they most want to use. Shapeshifters might need time to learn how to embody a new form, sometimes leading to an uncanny appearance or behavior until they’re accustomed to their body.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/shapeshifter/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/vampire",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Vampire",
      "slug": "vampire",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "VAMPIRE",
        "lineStart": 2738,
        "lineEnd": 2757,
        "pdfPageStart": 45,
        "pdfPageEnd": 45
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Fangs",
          "rulesText": "Make an attack using a trait of your choice to bite a target within Melee range. On a success, deal d6 physical damage using your Proficiency."
        },
        {
          "name": "Feed",
          "rulesText": "On a successful “Fangs” attack against a creature that can bleed, you can mark a Stress to feed. Place a number of tokens on this card equal to the number of Hit Points the target marks. You can hold up to 6 tokens at a time. Before you make an action roll, you can spend a token to make your Fear Die a d20. When you take a long rest, remove a token. While there are no tokens on this card, you make action and reaction rolls with disadvantage."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "Who did you have to leave behind when you became a vampire, and why was it so hard to let them go?",
        "You once killed an innocent for their blood. What characteristic of theirs do you notice most in others?",
        "What does your vampirisim make you particularly vulnerable to?",
        "Who were you able to save using your vampiric power? What happened, and how do they feel about you now?",
        "How does your hunger for blood change you?",
        "The vampire who sired you still has a hold over you. What’s their name, and what do they want you to do for them?"
      ],
      "description": "Vampires are undead creatures with sharp fangs who feed on the blood of the living. They aren’t born, but created—sired by another vampire through a bite and exchange of blood, transformed through an occult ritual, or changed through other mysterious methods. Because those who become vampires die and come back as supernatural entities, they must consume the blood—the life essence—of others to survive.\n\nAt first glance, many vampires are indistinguishable from typical members of their ancestry. Beyond their telltale fangs, they can be affected by their vampirism in myriad subtle or conspicuous ways, displaying traits such as sallow skin, visible veins, eyes that shine in the dark, or, in some cases, enhanced beauty. Depending on the nature of their vampiric origin, individuals may stop aging at the time of their transformation and remain vibrant and powerful long after their cohorts have succumbed to the ravages of time. No matter their appearance, all vampires possess enhanced abilities that can result in a terrifying end for their targets.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/vampire/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/transformations/werewolf",
      "@type": "Transformation",
      "name": "Werewolf",
      "slug": "werewolf",
      "sourceLocator": {
        "chapter": "CORE MATERIALS",
        "heading": "WEREWOLF",
        "lineStart": 2759,
        "lineEnd": 2778,
        "pdfPageStart": 45,
        "pdfPageEnd": 45
      },
      "features": [
        {
          "name": "Wolf Form",
          "rulesText": "When you mark 1 or more Hit Points, you can mark a Stress to enter your Wolf Form. While in this form, you gain a 1d10 bonus to attack and damage rolls. When you roll with Hope while in Wolf Form, you must mark a Stress. Your Wolf Form lasts until you go into your “Howling Rampage” or take a rest."
        },
        {
          "name": "Howling Rampage",
          "rulesText": "When you mark your last Stress while in Wolf Form, you go into a rampage. Roll a number of d20s equal to your tier and deal that much physical damage to all creatures within Very Close range, then drop out of Wolf Form."
        }
      ],
      "questions": [
        "Someone is hunting you. Who is it, and why do they want you dead?",
        "What were you afraid of before you became a werewolf, and how has your new power changed that fear?",
        "You hear your wolf form in your head as if it were another entity. What lie does it tell you about yourself, and why are you beginning to believe it?",
        "What led you to kill someone you love during your rampage?",
        "Who cursed you to become a werewolf?",
        "You were once entreated to join a pack of other werewolves. How did you respond?"
      ],
      "description": "Werewolves are creatures who transform into large supernatural wolves. While some are born werewolves, others undergo this transformation because of a curse, an infection through another werewolf’s bite, or another unnatural cause. Werewolves look like typical members of their ancestry until they shift, whereupon they transform into a massive otherworldly wolf. Some might even take on a shape that is an amalgamation of their ancestry and their wolf form. Stories describe werewolves shifting with the phases of the moon, though an individual’s experience with this change varies based on the cause of their transformation.\n\nWerewolves often struggle to control their hunting instinct and violence while in their wolf form, and they must have immense self-discipline, use significant energy, or resort to fleeing or locking themselves away to avoid harming their loved ones. The longer a werewolf has experience with their condition, the more likely they are to have developed methods of controlling themselves.",
      "htmlPage": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/records/transformations/werewolf/"
      },
      "source": {
        "@id": "https://cheeleong.dev/daggerheart-system-json/objects/sources/daggerheart-srd-2-0"
      }
    },
